          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": false,
          "prompt": "You are the SECURITY reviewer for a code review of the git branch `ralph/design` against base `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria.\n\nThe project is `talaria`, a Go CLI (cobra) that drives system `curl` to call APIs described by OpenAPI/Swagger specs. THE PRODUCT IS A CREDENTIAL FIREWALL: read `docs/design/DESIGN.md` §5a and §3 principle 0 first — \"no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value\". Secrets must be structural `SecretRef` values everywhere except `internal/curl` at exec time, where they are written to a `curl -K -` config document on curl's stdin (never argv).\n\nGet the diff with:\n  git diff main...HEAD -- '*.go' ':!*_test.go'\nThe whole branch is essentially new code (~10k lines of non-test Go). Read the actual source files, don't just skim the diff.\n\nNon-test source files (all new on this branch):\ncmd/talaria/{history,run,call,describe,root,auth,list,uses,search,version,main}.go\ninternal/operation/{search,index,extract,operation}.go\ninternal/gen/{gen,fixtures}.go\ninternal/request/{build,request,body}.go\ninternal/output/{schema,junit,render,output}.go\ninternal/curl/{config,exec,render,version,sweep,procgroup_unix,procgroup_other}.go\ninternal/corpus/{store,entry,lock_unix,lock_other}.go\ninternal/config/{auth,config}.go\ninternal/secret/{response,secret,redact}.go\ninternal/validate/validate.go\ninternal/spec/{source,convert,load,spec}.go\ninternal/canary/surfaces.go\ninternal/clierr/clierr.go\n\nYour checklist:\n- Credential leakage on ANY output surface: json/pretty/tsv output, dry-run curl, emitted curl, error messages (internal/clierr), history entries, corpus files, JUnit reports, warnings. Trace every path where a resolved secret value could become a string.\n- Secrets in argv: grep for anywhere a secret could reach exec.Command args or the process environment visible to others.\n- Injection: is user input parameterized in shell calls / curl config documents? Can a header value, param value, or body inject a new directive line into the `curl -K -` config document (newline injection)? Can a spec-supplied value do so?\n- Path traversal: spec loading, cache paths, --fixtures dir, corpus store paths, temp files. Are symlinks resolved? Is the cache key attacker-controllable?\n- File permissions: config file 0600, corpus/history store perms, temp body files (design says 0600 and delete after exec).\n- URL/SSRF-ish concerns: base-url scheme restriction to http/https, userinfo credentials rejection, redirect following.\n- Secrets committed in the diff (check testdata and fixtures too — canary values are intentional, real credentials are not).\n- Response redaction: Set-Cookie, configured JSON body paths, arrays and nested arrays.\n- TOCTOU / race on temp files or the corpus lock.\n\nReport ONLY issues introduced by this branch, each with an exact `file:line`. For each, give: title, severity (CRIT = real exploitable bug/leak/broken functionality; WARN = missing validation, hardening gap, spec drift; INFO = style/docs), a description of what is wrong and why it matters, and a concrete suggested fix.\n\nBe rigorous about severity — verify a leak actually reaches an output surface before calling it CRIT. Read the tests if you need to check whether a path is already covered. You may run `go test ./...` and write throwaway scratch files under /tmp to prove a finding, but DO NOT modify any file in the repo.\n\nReturn your findings as a numbered list in that format. If you found nothing at a severity, say so explicitly."
        },
        "caller": {
          "type": "direct"
        }
      }
