          "prompt": "You are the SPEC-COMPLIANCE reviewer for a code review of git branch `ralph/design` against base `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria.\n\nThe specification is `docs/design/DESIGN.md` (513 lines) — read it in full. Supporting conventions/docs that are also part of the deliverable and part of the diff: `README.md`, `AGENT.md`, `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`, and `docs/research/2026-08-02-design-review.md`.\n\nScope built on this branch: Phases 1-4 of DESIGN.md §7 (list, describe, search, uses, call --dry-run, real execution + credential firewall + history, response validation, `run` smoke mode). Phases 5-8 (recording proxy, twin serve, stateful twin, fault injection) are deliberately NOT built — do NOT report their absence as a finding; there is an explicit decision gate after Phase 4.\n\nGet the diff:\n  git diff main...HEAD --stat\n  git diff main...HEAD -- '*.go' ':!*_test.go'\n  git diff main...HEAD -- README.md AGENT.md docs/\n\nYour checklist, against DESIGN.md:\n- §4 CLI surface: does every documented command, subcommand, and flag exist with the documented name and semantics? (list/describe/search/uses/call/history/history show/history replay/run/auth check/version, and every flag listed under them). Flags that exist but behave differently than documented are findings; so are documented flags that are missing.\n- §4 Exit codes table: verify each of 0/1/2/3/4/5 is actually produced in the circumstances described. Especially: HTTP 4xx/5xx is exit 0 for `call`; exit 5 for missing credential; exit 2 for usage error with stderr JSON listing valid options; exit 4 only with --fail-on-error or `run`.\n- §4 \"Output shape for call\" sketch: does the real JSON envelope match (schema field \"talaria/v1\", request.curl, request.method/url/headers, response.status/headers/body/timing_ms, validation.status_documented/body_valid/errors)?\n- §3 principles: --output json on EVERY command; default pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped; never prompt/page/interactive; errors to stderr as structured JSON with valid alternatives; versioned schema.\n- §5 architecture boundary rules: `operation`, `validate`, `gen` must not import `curl` or `twin`. Verify with grep of import blocks.\n- §5 Auth: env var conventions TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER / TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC / TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_<SCHEME>; profile file at ~/.config/talaria/config.yaml mode 0600; v1 scope bearer/basic/apikey in header/query/cookie.\n- §5a: symbolic curl, redaction list (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization, *api*key*, *token*, *secret* + user-extensible), `curl -K -`, stdin ownership rule, temp-file fallback deleted after exec, query-string key warn-once on stderr, canary CI suite covering every surface named in §5a.\n- §5 curl >= 7.70 hard floor preflight; `--write-out '%{json}'`.\n- §5a \"Test data for run mode\" priority order: spec example/examples -> --fixtures dir matched by operationId -> gen.\n- §2/§3 progressive disclosure: describe uses the compact `name*: (string) description` form, not raw JSON Schema.\n- Doc drift: does README.md or AGENT.md document behaviour that the code does not implement, or vice versa? Run the documented examples where cheap. A README that promises a flag that doesn't exist is a CRIT (agents read it).\n\nReport ONLY gaps introduced/left by this branch, each with an exact `file:line` (cite the code file, or the doc file when the doc is what's wrong). For each: title, severity (CRIT = unmet design-doc requirement / documented-but-missing behaviour / contract mismatch; WARN = drift or partial implementation; INFO = wording), description, concrete suggested fix.\n\nYou may build and run the binary (`go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria`) and exercise commands to verify — the repo has testdata specs under cmd/talaria/testdata/ and internal/*/testdata/. DO NOT modify any file in the repo.\n\nReturn findings as a numbered list. If a checklist item is fully satisfied, don't report it."
