    "role": "assistant",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "tool_use",
        "id": "toolu_01FwfcsSfqLDdgcgciczyGod",
        "name": "Agent",
        "input": {
          "description": "Concurrency review",
          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": false,
          "prompt": "You are the CONCURRENCY reviewer for a code review of git branch `ralph/design` against base `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria.\n\nProject: `talaria`, a Go CLI that shells out to system `curl` via os/exec, stores a history \"corpus\" on disk, and runs smoke tests over many operations. Design doc: docs/design/DESIGN.md.\n\nGet the diff:\n  git diff main...HEAD -- '*.go' ':!*_test.go'\n\nFocus areas (read the real files, not just the diff):\n- internal/curl/exec.go, internal/curl/procgroup_unix.go, internal/curl/procgroup_other.go, internal/curl/sweep.go — subprocess lifecycle. Signal handling was added by commit 94a68c3 \"fix: cancel curl on a signal instead of orphaning it\". Verify: is every spawned curl guaranteed to be reaped? Can a signal handler goroutine leak or outlive the command? Is the signal.Notify channel stopped? Is the process group killed correctly, and is there a race between Start() and the kill? Are stdout/stderr pipes always closed and fully drained (deadlock risk on a large response with a full pipe buffer)? Is the temp body file always removed on the signal path?\n- internal/corpus/store.go, lock_unix.go, lock_other.go — the history store. Commit e0cceae \"fix: serialise history appends so trim cannot destroy entries\". Verify the flock/locking protocol: is the lock held across the full read-modify-write? Is the file descriptor closed on every path including errors? Is the fallback (lock_other.go) safe or silently a no-op that loses data? Is a concurrent `history` read against a concurrent append able to observe a torn/partial entry? Is the write atomic (temp + rename) and does rename happen within the lock?\n- cmd/talaria/run.go — smoke testing many operations. Is there any parallelism? If yes: data races on shared result slices/maps, unbounded goroutine fan-out, per-operation timeout enforcement, context cancellation honoured, ordering determinism of the report.\n- cmd/talaria/history.go, call.go — context and timeout propagation. Is context cancellation actually plumbed into exec? Is --timeout enforced with both connect and total bounds?\n- Errors swallowed instead of propagated anywhere in the above.\n- Any use of `go func` in the whole non-test diff: is every one guaranteed to exit?\n\nRun `go test -race ./...` and report any race or failure it surfaces (that is strong evidence). You may also write throwaway reproduction programs under /tmp. DO NOT modify any file in the repo.\n\nReport only issues introduced by this branch, each with an exact `file:line`. For each: title, severity (CRIT = real race/leak/deadlock/data-loss; WARN = fragile pattern or missing bound; INFO = style), description of the failure scenario (concrete: what interleaving, what input), and a concrete suggested fix.\n\nBe honest about severity — a theoretical race that cannot occur because the code is single-threaded is not CRIT. Verify before you claim.\n\nReturn findings as a numbered list. If you found nothing at a severity, say so explicitly."
        },
        "caller": {
          "type": "direct"
        }
      }
    ],
    "stop_reason": null,
