{
  "type": "user",
  "message": {
    "role": "user",
    "content": [
      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01VYBhBAyv7sXkMPYwjCyB47",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "1\t# talaria — operating manual for agents\n2\t\n3\tYou drive an HTTP API through this binary. It reads an OpenAPI/Swagger spec and runs the calls\n4\tthrough curl. **You never see credentials.** Values are resolved inside the process at the moment\n5\tof the request and appear in no output — not in JSON, not in errors, not in the curl it prints,\n6\tnot in history. You work with credential *names*.\n7\t\n8\tThere is no `--show-secrets` flag and no way to reveal a value. Asking a human to paste one into\n9\tyour context is not a supported workflow — see [Credentials](#credentials) for what to do instead.\n10\t\n11\t## The loop\n12\t\n13\t```sh\n14\ttalaria list ./openapi.yaml                       # what operations exist\n15\ttalaria search ./openapi.yaml invoice             # find one by concept\n16\ttalaria describe ./openapi.yaml getPet            # its params, body, responses\n17\ttalaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42 --dry-run   # check the request\n18\ttalaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42             # send it\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tDo not read the spec file yourself. It is often megabytes; these commands are the point.\n22\t\n23\t## The spec\n24\t\n25\tEvery spec-reading command takes it from the first of: the positional argument, `--spec`, then\n26\t`$TALARIA_SPEC`. Export `TALARIA_SPEC` once and drop the argument. A file path or an `http(s)`\n27\tURL, OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0.\n28\t\n29\tWith two positional arguments the spec is first (`talaria describe ./openapi.yaml getPet`); with\n30\tone, the argument is the operationId.\n31\t\n32\t## Commands\n33\t\n34\t| Command | What it gives you |\n35\t|---|---|\n36\t| `talaria list [spec] [--tag t]` | one line per operation: method, path, operationId, summary |\n37\t| `talaria search [spec] <query> [--kind operation\\|schema\\|param]` | substring match across operations, schemas and params |\n38\t| `talaria describe [spec] <operationId> [--depth n]` | one operation's params, request body and responses |\n39\t| `talaria uses [spec] <schema>` | every operation that touches a component schema |\n40\t| `talaria call [spec] <operationId>` | build and send one request; `--dry-run` builds only, `--fail-on-error` exits 4 on an HTTP or spec violation |\n41\t| `talaria run [spec] [--tag t] [--operation id]` | smoke-test many operations at once and report one line each |\n42\t| `talaria auth check [spec]` | which credentials the spec needs and whether they are set |\n43\t| `talaria history [--operation id] [--since 1h] [--status 4xx] [--source call\\|run\\|replay]` | what has already been called |\n44\t| `talaria history show <id\\|n>` | one recorded request/response in full |\n45\t| `talaria history replay <id\\|n>` | send a recorded request again |\n46\t| `talaria version` | the binary's version |\n47\t\n48\tOperations the spec did not name get a synthesised operationId (`GET /pets/{petId}` →\n49\t`getPetsByPetId`). It is stable and callable.\n50\t\n51\t## Calling\n52\t\n53\t```sh\n54\ttalaria call getPet --param petId=42 --query verbose=true --header X-Trace=abc\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t- `--param name=value` binds a parameter the operation declares, in any location. `--query` and\n58\t  `--header` add ones it does not. All three repeat.\n59\t- `--body` takes a literal, `@file`, or `-` for stdin. Content type comes from your\n60\t  `--header Content-Type`, else from the operation's declared media type.\n61\t- Every binding problem is reported at once. Fix them in one edit, not one per run.\n62\t- `--base-url` overrides the spec's server. `--profile` selects a named profile from the user's\n63\t  config file. Only `http` and `https` are accepted, from either source — a spec's own\n64\t  `servers[0].url` is untrusted input, so a `file://` or `gopher://` base is a usage error\n65\t  (exit 2) rather than a request. So is a base URL carrying credentials —\n66\t  `http://user:password@host` — because a base URL is copied whole into the emitted `curl` and\n67\t  into history; set `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC=user:password` instead.\n68\t- Every request is bounded: 10s to connect, 30s in total, `--timeout <seconds>` to change the\n69\t  total. An API that stops answering exits 1 with curl's status 28 in the message — talaria\n70\t  never hangs waiting for one.\n71\t- Interrupting talaria (Ctrl-C, SIGTERM) cancels the request in flight and exits 1 with a\n72\t  message saying it was cancelled, not that it timed out. An interrupted mutation is one talaria\n73\t  stopped sending; the attempt is still in history, with no response block.\n74\t\n75\tThe response block carries status, headers, timing and body; a JSON body is embedded as JSON, so\n76\tone parse gets you the fields. **An HTTP 4xx or 5xx exits 0** — it is a successful observation.\n77\tRead `response.status`; do not infer failure from the exit code.\n78\t\n79\t### The validation block\n80\t\n81\tEvery executed call also carries `validation`:\n82\t\n83\t```json\n84\t\"validation\": { \"status_documented\": true, \"content_type_documented\": true, \"body_valid\": true, \"errors\": [] }\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tThe three booleans answer independently, so an undocumented 500 is distinguishable from a\n88\tdocumented 200 that came back with the wrong shape. Each schema failure is one entry in\n89\t`errors`, with the JSONPath of the offending field.\n90\t\n91\tA violation is an observation too: it still exits 0. `--fail-on-error` is how you ask for the\n92\tother behaviour — it exits **4** when the response is an HTTP error or violates the spec, and\n93\tprints the same envelope either way. Use it when you are testing; leave it off when you are\n94\texploring. A dry run has no validation block, because nothing came back.\n95\t\n96\tValidation reads the *redacted* response, so a field redacted by configuration is checked as\n97\t`<redacted>` and may report a violation the server did not commit.\n98\t\n99\t### Mutations are gated\n100\t\n101\t`GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` run. Every other method needs `--allow-mutations` and exits 2 without\n102\tit. The gate is checked before the request is built, and `--dry-run` does not exempt it.\n103\t\n104\tNeeding `--allow-mutations` is a decision, not a formality. Do not add it to a command that\n105\tfailed on it without saying what you are about to change and on which host. The sanctioned way\n106\tto exercise a `DELETE` is against a disposable environment — point `--base-url` at one. (The\n107\tbuilt-in digital twin that will make this cheap is designed but not yet shipped.)\n108\t\n109\t## Smoke testing\n110\t\n111\t`run` is `call` over many operations: it answers \"is this whole API behaving?\" in one command.\n112\t\n113\t```sh\n114\ttalaria run ./openapi.yaml --tag pets --operation getPet --base-url http://localhost:9000\n115\t```\n116\t\n117\t- `--tag` and `--operation` both repeat, and combining them is the **union**, not the\n118\t  intersection. No filter at all runs the whole spec. A filter that matches nothing exits 2\n119\t  rather than passing with nothing tested.\n120\t- Operations run one at a time, in spec order.\n121\t- Request data comes from the spec's own `example` first, then a file in `--fixtures dir/` named\n122\t  `<operationId>.json`, then generation from the schema. A fixture is `{\"params\": {…},\n123\t  \"headers\": {…}, \"body\": {…}}`; every field is optional.\n124\t- `--report json|pretty|tsv|junit` is `run`'s own format flag and overrides `--output`. `junit`\n125\t  writes a JUnit XML suite — one `<testcase>` per operation, `<failure>` or `<skipped>` carrying\n126\t  the reason — for a CI job to collect. It is a `--report` value only; `--output junit` exits 2.\n127\t- `--timeout` bounds each operation, not the suite. An endpoint that never answers becomes one\n128\t  failed line and the report still arrives.\n129\t\n130\tEach operation reports `passed`, `failed` or `skipped`, plus a `reason` for the last two:\n131\t\n132\t```json\n133\t{\"operation_id\":\"getPet\",\"method\":\"GET\",\"path\":\"/pets/{petId}\",\"outcome\":\"passed\",\"status\":200,\n134\t \"timing_ms\":12,\"validation\":{\"status_documented\":true,\"body_valid\":true,\"errors\":[]}}\n135\t```\n136\t\n137\t**A skip is not a failure.** A `DELETE` left alone without `--allow-mutations`, or an operation\n138\twhose required parameter nothing could supply, is correct behaviour — read the `reason` before\n139\treacting. The `summary` block carries `total`, `passed`, `failed` and `skipped`.\n140\t\n141\tExit codes follow `call`: failures exit 0 unless you pass `--fail-on-error`, which makes them\n142\texit 4. A missing credential exits **5** whichever flags you passed — that operation was never\n143\ttested, so ask a human to export the variable named in its `reason`. Every request `run` makes is\n144\trecorded in history with `\"source\": \"run\"`, and `history --source call` filters them back out.\n145\t\n146\t## Credentials\n147\t\n148\tYou cannot read a credential and you do not need to. talaria maps each security scheme the spec\n149\tdeclares onto an environment variable, and carries the name:\n150\t\n151\t| Scheme in the spec | Variable |\n152\t|---|---|\n153\t| `type: http`, `scheme: bearer` | `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` |\n154\t| `type: http`, `scheme: basic` | `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC` (`user:password`) |\n155\t| `type: apiKey`, named `petKey` | `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY` (scheme name, upper-cased) |\n156\t\n157\t```sh\n158\ttalaria auth check ./openapi.yaml\n159\t{\"scheme\":\"bearerAuth\",\"source\":\"env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\",\"present\":true}\n160\t```\n161\t\n162\t`present` is a lookup; the value is never read. A profile can redirect a scheme to a different\n163\tvariable, and `auth check` reports whichever one is actually in force.\n164\t\n165\t**When a credential is missing** (exit 5, or `present: false`), the fix is one sentence to a\n166\thuman — name the variable and stop:\n167\t\n168\t> This API needs a bearer token. Export it before I retry:\n169\t> `export TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=…`. Do not paste the value to me; talaria reads it from the\n170\t> environment and I never see it.\n171\t\n172\tNever ask for the value, never suggest putting a literal token in a config file (talaria refuses\n173\tthem), and never try to read one out of the environment yourself. A credential you supply inline\n174\t— `--header X-Api-Key=sk-live-…` — is redacted by name in the output and in the emitted curl,\n175\tso it is not copy-pasteable either.\n176\t\n177\tThe emitted curl references variables symbolically:\n178\t\n179\t```\n180\tcurl -q -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\" 'https://api.example.com/v1/pets/42'\n181\t```\n182\t\n183\tThat command is runnable in a shell where the variable is set and useless to anyone else. Quote\n184\tit freely in reports. The leading `-q` is the one talaria itself passes: it stops curl reading\n185\t`~/.curlrc`, whose directives would otherwise apply to the request carrying the credential.\n186\t\n187\tOne thing redaction cannot fix: an API key that belongs in the *query string* travels in the URL\n188\tand lands in server access logs. talaria warns once on stderr. Report the warning; it is a\n189\tproperty of the API, not a bug you can work around.\n190\t\n191\tResponse bodies are redacted only at the OAuth2 token fields and whatever JSON paths the user\n192\tconfigured. A login or token-issuing endpoint is for a human to run, not for you.\n193\t\n194\t## Output\n195\t\n196\t`--output json|pretty|tsv` on every command. Piped output defaults to `json`, so you normally get\n197\tJSON without asking. Every payload carries `\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"` — branch on it if you cache\n198\tparsing logic.\n199\t\n200\tErrors go to **stderr** as one line of JSON in the same envelope:\n201\t\n202\t```json\n203\t{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"unknown operation \\\"getpet\\\"\",\"valid_alternatives\":[\"getPet\",\"getPets\"]}}\n204\t```\n205\t\n206\tWhen `valid_alternatives` is present it is the complete set of right answers. Pick from it rather\n207\tthan guessing again.\n208\t\n209\t## Exit codes\n210\t\n211\t| Code | Meaning | What to do |\n212\t|---|---|---|\n213\t| 0 | Success. A 4xx/5xx response is still success | read `response.status` |\n214\t| 1 | The request could not be completed: network, TLS, curl itself | check the host and `--base-url`; retrying once is reasonable |\n215\t| 2 | Usage error: unknown command or operation, a group named without its subcommand (`talaria auth`), missing parameter, bad flag, or a mutation without `--allow-mutations` | fix the invocation using `valid_alternatives` and the message |\n216\t| 3 | The spec could not be read or parsed | check the path or URL; do not retry unchanged |\n217\t| 4 | With `--fail-on-error`: the response was an HTTP error or violated the spec | read the `validation` block and `response.status` on stdout; report what failed |\n218\t| 5 | A required credential is not set | tell a human which variable to export; do not retry until they have |\n219\t\n220\t## History\n221\t\n222\tEvery real call is recorded, redacted at write time, newest first. Use it instead of re-issuing a\n223\trequest to remember what happened:\n224\t\n225\t```sh\n226\ttalaria history --operation getPet --status 4xx\n227\ttalaria history show 2026-08-02T21:40:11.183204Z\n228\t```\n229\t\n230\tEach entry has an `id` and an index, and `show` and `replay` take either. **Use the id.** The\n231\tindex is the entry's position in the store, counted fresh on every read, so it shifts every time\n232\tanything is recorded — and `call`, `run` and `replay` all record, replay included. Two replays in\n233\ta row by index re-issue the same entry twice; two by id re-issue the two you asked for. The index\n234\tis stable only under filters, which do not renumber it. An entry recorded by an older talaria has\n235\tno id and lists as `-`; only its index can name it.\n236\t\n237\t`talaria history replay <id|n>` re-sends an entry and records the result as a new one;\n238\treplaying a mutation needs `--allow-mutations` too. Dry runs are never recorded. Recording is off\n239\twhere a profile says so, or everywhere under `TALARIA_HISTORY=off` — if history is empty, that is\n240\tusually why.\n241\t\n242\tCredentials are stored as names, so nothing in history can be read back into a value.\n243\t\n244\t## Rules\n245\t\n246\t1. Never ask a human for a credential value. Ask them to export a named variable.\n247\t2. Never add `--allow-mutations` silently.\n248\t3. Treat a 4xx as data, not as a crash. Exit 0 means talaria worked.\n249\t4. Prefer `describe` over reading the spec, and `history` over repeating a call.\n250\t5. Use `--dry-run` when you are unsure what a request will look like. It costs nothing.\n251\t"
      }
    ]
  },
