          "prompt": "You are the INTEGRATION reviewer for a code review of git branch `ralph/design` against base `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria.\n\nProject: `talaria`, a Go CLI. The pipeline is:\n  spec load (internal/spec) -> operation model (internal/operation) -> request binding (internal/request)\n  -> secret refs (internal/secret) -> curl config document + render (internal/curl) -> exec system curl\n  -> response -> validate (internal/validate) -> redact (internal/secret) -> corpus/history (internal/corpus)\n  -> output envelope (internal/output) -> cmd/talaria commands.\nDesign doc: docs/design/DESIGN.md. Also read AGENT.md and README.md — they are the agent-facing contract.\n\nGet the diff:\n  git diff main...HEAD -- '*.go' ':!*_test.go'\n  git diff main...HEAD --stat\n\nYour job is to verify data actually flows end to end across component boundaries — VERIFY, do not assume:\n- Does the Request built by internal/request reach internal/curl with all fields intact (path params, query, headers, body, media type, auth)? Any field silently dropped between layers?\n- Does what `--dry-run` renders match what real execution actually sends? (commits 2d92e67 and f176666 touched exactly this — HEAD method, body flag, media type. Check for remaining divergence: query encoding, repeated params, cookies, form data vs json, --data-binary vs --data.)\n- Does `history replay` faithfully re-issue the recorded request? Commit 5aa07bf gave entries a stable id. Verify: does the id survive a trim? Does `history show <n>` and `history replay <n>` address the SAME entry (index vs id confusion)? What happens on a replay after the corpus is trimmed or an entry is removed?\n- Does the redaction applied at corpus write time survive round-trip through `history show`, and can replay still reconstruct a working request from a redacted entry (the credential must be re-resolved, not replayed as \"<redacted>\")? This is the highest-risk seam.\n- Swagger 2.0 -> OpenAPI 3.x conversion (internal/spec/convert.go): do converted specs carry security schemes, body params, and consumes/produces through to the operation model and on to the request? Test with internal/spec/testdata/swagger2-apikey.json and internal/e2e/testdata/e2e-api-2.0.json.\n- `run` mode: does the examples -> fixtures -> gen test-data priority chain actually feed the request builder, and do per-operation failures land in the JSON and JUnit reports correctly (counts, failure messages, exit code)?\n- Validation: does internal/validate receive the real response bytes and content type, and does its verdict reach the `validation` block in the output envelope and the exit code?\n- Environment parity: does anything behave differently under test than at runtime? Look for test-only hooks, injected fakes that bypass real code paths, TTY detection, env-var lookups stubbed in tests, or a fake curl used in tests that hides a real bug. `internal/e2e/e2e_test.go` and `internal/canary/canary_test.go` are the integration suites — check whether they exercise the real binary and real curl or a substitute.\n- Are the CI workflow (.github/workflows/ci.yml) checks the same as what a developer runs locally?\n\nBuild and drive the real binary to verify: `go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria` then exercise it against testdata specs and, where an HTTP endpoint is needed, a scratch server you write under /tmp. DO NOT modify any file in the repo.\n\nReport only issues introduced by this branch, each with an exact `file:line`. For each: title, severity (CRIT = data does not reach the next component / broken functionality / wrong result; WARN = fragile seam, missing coverage of a real path, environment divergence; INFO = minor), description with a concrete failure scenario, and a concrete suggested fix.\n\nReturn findings as a numbered list. If you found nothing at a severity, say so explicitly."
