{
  "type": "user",
  "message": {
    "role": "user",
    "content": [
      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Mu3fEMrGdYL7HPNGSwDQse",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "const FileMode os.FileMode = 0o600\n\n// Config is the parsed profile file. The zero value is a usable empty config,\n// which is what a missing file loads as.\ntype Config struct {\n\tProfiles map[string]*Profile `yaml:\"profiles\"`\n\t// Redact extends the credential firewall's built-in lists. It is file-wide\n\t// rather than per-profile: which fields of an API's responses are secret is\n\t// a property of the API, not of which environment you point at.\n\tRedact Redact `yaml:\"redact\"`\n\n\t// path is where this config was read from, so an error about a profile can\n\t// name the file to edit.\n\tpath string\n}\n\n// Redact is the user's extension of the built-in redaction lists (§5a). Both\n// lists only ever add: nothing here can stop talaria redacting an Authorization\n// header, because a firewall a config file can switch off is not one.\ntype Redact struct {\n\t// Headers are extra header-name globs, e.g. x-session-*. They apply to\n\t// request and response headers alike.\n\tHeaders []string `yaml:\"headers\"`\n\t// BodyPaths are dotted JSON paths into a *response* body, e.g. data.token.\n\t// They are how a login endpoint's `access_token` stays out of an agent's\n\t// context; §5a is explicit that talaria cannot infer them.\n\tBodyPaths []string `yaml:\"body-paths\"`\n}\n\n// Profile is one named environment: where to send requests, what to send with\n// them, and which credentials to use.\ntype Profile struct {\n\t// Name is the key this profile appeared under. It is filled in on lookup\n\t// rather than read from the file.\n\tName string `yaml:\"-\"`\n\t// BaseURL overrides the spec's servers[0].url. It loses to --base-url.\n\tBaseURL string `yaml:\"base-url\"`\n\t// Headers are sent with every request made under this profile.\n\tHeaders map[string]string `yaml:\"headers\"`\n\t// Auth maps a security scheme name from the spec to an environment-variable\n\t// reference such as ${STAGING_TOKEN}. It holds references, never values:\n\t// see Resolve.\n\tAuth map[string]string `yaml:\"auth\"`\n\t// History is this profile's half of the recording opt-out. It is per-profile\n\t// rather than file-wide because \"record what I do here\" is a property of the\n\t// environment: a production profile is exactly the one you might not want a\n---README---\n204:The same file extends the redaction lists. Both keys only ever *add* — nothing in a config file\n205:can stop talaria redacting an `Authorization` header:\n206-\n207-```yaml\n208:redact:\n209-  headers:\n210-    - x-session-*\n211-  body-paths:\n212-    - data.token\n213-```\n214-\n215-`headers` are globs over the header name, applied to request and response headers alike — and, as\n216-the built-in list is, to query parameters and cookies, which are credential locations under\n217-another name. They hide the value everywhere it is displayed: `request.headers`, the emitted\n218-curl, `--dry-run`, pretty output and history. They go on top\n219-of the built-in list (`Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Set-Cookie`,\n220-`*api*key*`, `*token*`, `*secret*`). `body-paths` are dotted JSON paths into a *response* body,\n--\n285:`<redacted:env:NAME>` — that field is read, not run. The real value goes on the wire and\n286-appears in no output surface at all.\n287-\n288-A credential you supply yourself is treated the same way. `--header X-Api-Key=sk-live-…`,\n289:a header from a profile, or a bound parameter whose name matches the redaction list all\n290:display as `<redacted>` in the request block *and* in the emitted curl, while still being sent\n291-verbatim. The name decides, not where the value came from — so the emitted command for a\n292-literal credential is deliberately not copy-pasteable, and there is no flag that makes it so.\n293-\n294-Alongside it, `response` carries the status, headers, timing and body; a JSON body is embedded\n295-as JSON rather than as a quoted string, so an agent parses the envelope once instead of twice.\n296-Pretty output prints the request line, the curl, and `200 OK in 143ms` — response headers stay\n297-in `--output json`, since a `Set-Cookie` does not belong in someone's scrollback unasked.\n298-\n299:What comes back is redacted too, within the limits of what a tool can know. Response headers go\n300:through the same name matcher as request headers, so `Set-Cookie` reads `<redacted>` by default;\n301:response *bodies* are redacted only at the JSON paths you configure, plus the OAuth2 token\n302-fields (`access_token`, `refresh_token`, `id_token`). A body in which nothing matched is\n303-returned byte for byte, so what you read is what the server sent. This is a mitigation, not a\n304-solution: talaria cannot tell a secret field from an ordinary one by looking at it, so an\n305-auth-issuing endpoint is for a human, not for an agent.\n306-\n307:One thing redaction cannot reach: an API key that belongs in the *query string* travels in the\n308-URL, and URLs are written to server access logs. talaria warns once on stderr when an operation\n309-sends one — naming the parameter and the environment variable, never the value.\n310-\n311-Two rules apply before anything is sent:\n312-\n313-- **Read-only by default.** `GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` need no flag; every other method\n314-  requires `--allow-mutations` and exits 2 without it. The gate is checked before the request\n315-  is even built, and `--dry-run` does not exempt it, so the rule is learnable without a\n316-  network round trip.\n317-- **`--dry-run` sends nothing.** It prints the same request block a real call would, minus the\n318-  response, and exits 0. The `curl` field is identical either way.\n319-\n--\n351:Validation runs on the **redacted** response, not on the raw one. Validation errors quote the\n352-content they rejected, so a validator fed the raw body would be the one place a secret\n353:reappeared after redaction removed it. The cost is that a redacted field is validated as\n354:`<redacted>`, so redacting a field the schema constrains reports a violation the server did not\n355-commit — a visible, correctable error, which is the better failure of the two.\n356-",
        "is_error": false
      }
    ]
  },
