        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "39-| `talaria uses [spec] <schema>` | every operation that touches a component schema |\n40-| `talaria call [spec] <operationId>` | build and send one request; `--dry-run` builds only, `--fail-on-error` exits 4 on an HTTP or spec violation |\n41-| `talaria run [spec] [--tag t] [--operation id]` | smoke-test many operations at once and report one line each |\n42:| `talaria auth check [spec]` | which credentials the spec needs and whether they are set |\n43-| `talaria history [--operation id] [--since 1h] [--status 4xx] [--source call\\|run\\|replay]` | what has already been called |\n44-| `talaria history show <id\\|n>` | one recorded request/response in full |\n45-| `talaria history replay <id\\|n>` | send a recorded request again |\n46-| `talaria version` | the binary's version |\n47-\n48-Operations the spec did not name get a synthesised operationId (`GET /pets/{petId}` →\n49-`getPetsByPetId`). It is stable and callable.\n50-\n--\n155-| `type: apiKey`, named `petKey` | `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY` (scheme name, upper-cased) |\n156-\n157-```sh\n158:talaria auth check ./openapi.yaml\n159-{\"scheme\":\"bearerAuth\",\"source\":\"env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\",\"present\":true}\n160-```\n161-\n162-`present` is a lookup; the value is never read. A profile can redirect a scheme to a different\n163:variable, and `auth check` reports whichever one is actually in force.\n164-\n165-**When a credential is missing** (exit 5, or `present: false`), the fix is one sentence to a\n166-human — name the variable and stop:\n167-\n168-> This API needs a bearer token. Export it before I retry:\n169-> `export TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=…`. Do not paste the value to me; talaria reads it from the\n170-> environment and I never see it.\n171-\n=====README=====\n5-\n6-> **Status: early implementation.** `talaria version`, `talaria list`, `talaria describe`,\n7:> `talaria search`, `talaria uses`, `talaria call`, `talaria auth check`,\n8-> `talaria history` and `talaria run` work so far; the rest of the command tree is scaffolded.\n9-> The [design document](docs/design/DESIGN.md) is the source of truth.\n10-\n11-Every API client — Postman, Insomnia, Bruno, curl itself — assumes the operator is a human who\n12-is entitled to see their own secrets. Hand an agent a Postman collection with an environment, a\n13-`.bru` file, or a shell with `$STRIPE_KEY` exported, and the agent has your key: in its context\n14-window, in the transcript, in the logs.\n15-\n16-**talaria is the trust boundary.** The agent operates on credential *names*; the binary\n17-resolves *values* at the last possible moment and never emits them — not in output, not in\n--\n229-\n230-```sh\n231:talaria auth check ./openapi.yaml\n232:talaria auth check ./openapi.yaml --profile staging --output json\n233-```\n234-\n235:`auth check` answers the one question an agent must be able to ask about credentials: is one\n236-there? It reports every security scheme the spec declares, the variable it comes from, and\n237-whether that variable is set — never what it is set to.\n238-\n239-```json\n240-{\"scheme\":\"bearerAuth\",\"source\":\"env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\",\"present\":true}\n241-```\n242-\n243-The source follows the same resolution `call` uses, so `--profile staging` reports the\n244-profile's variable (`env:STAGING_TOKEN`) rather than the convention's. Presence is tested with\n245-a lookup; the value is never read.",
        "is_error": false
      }
    ]
  },
