  "summary": "Repo unmodified. Findings below.\n\n## Spec-compliance findings — `ralph/design` vs `main`, against `docs/design/DESIGN.md`\n\n**Verified clean** (not reported): every §4 command/subcommand/flag exists with the documented name; all six exit codes reproduce under the documented circumstances (0 for HTTP 4xx/5xx, 1 network, 2 usage + stderr JSON with `valid_alternatives`, 3 spec parse, 4 only under `--fail-on-error`/`run`, 5 missing credential); the `call` envelope matches the §4 sketch field-for-field; `--output json|pretty|tsv` on every command with pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped; no prompts, pagers or `--verbose`; `operation`/`validate`/`gen` import neither `curl` nor `twin`; the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` conventions, `~/.config/talaria/config.yaml` 0600 enforcement and env-ref-only profile auth; symbolic curl, the full §5a redaction list (headers, query params, cookies, `Set-Cookie`, response body paths) applied at write time to history; `curl -q -K -`, stdin ownership, 0600 temp-file fallback with deferred delete, warn-once query-key stderr notice; curl ≥ 7.70 preflight and `--write-out '%{json}'`; the examples→fixtures→gen chain; `describe`'s compact `name*: (type) description` form with `[circular]`/`[max depth]`; the canary suite gating CI. `go test ./...` passes.\n\n---\n\n1. **`search` is exact-substring, not the \"fuzzy find\" §4 specifies** — WARN\n   `internal/operation/search.go:121` — matching is `strings.Contains(f.text, query)` on lower-cased text only. `docs/design/DESIGN.md:169` annotates the command `# fuzzy find across the spec`, and §4's justifying paragraph (`DESIGN.md:210-213`) rests on the agent knowing \"a *domain concept* … rather than an operationId\". Verified: `search search.yaml invoice` → 5 hits; `search search.yaml invoce` → `{\"results\":[]}`, exit 0. A typo, a plural/singular mismatch or a synonym silently yields \"this API has no such concept\". `README.md:120` documents the shipped substring behaviour, so DESIGN and README now contradict each other. **Fix:** either add edit-distance/subsequence fallback ranked below exact matches, or amend `DESIGN.md:169` to `# substring find across the spec` — this branch already amended DESIGN.md three times for resolved decisions, so leaving this one is the inconsistency.\n\n2. **§4's exit-4 row reads as \"`run` always exits 4\"; shipped `run` needs `--fail-on-error`** — WARN\n   `cmd/talaria/run.go:408` (`if failOnError && view.Summary.Failed > 0`) vs `docs/design/DESIGN.md:248` (`only with --fail-on-error / run`). Verified: `run` over a spec with a schema-violating response exits 0 without the flag, 4 with it. The behaviour is defensible and matches §4's own flag list (`DESIGN.md:196`) and `README.md:464`, but the exit-code table still reads the other way — an agent given only the table will branch wrong. **Fix:** amend `DESIGN.md:248` to `only with --fail-on-error (on call or run)`.\n\n3. **stderr mixes bare prose warnings with the JSON envelope** — WARN\n   `internal/secret/response.go:258` (`QueryKeyWarner.Warn`) and `cmd/talaria/history.go:687` (`warnUnreplayable`) both `fmt.Fprintf` unwrapped text. Verified on a call that both warns and fails:\n   ```\n   warning: this operation sends $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY in the query parameter \"api_key\"; …\n   {\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"…\"}}\n   ```\n   `AGENT.md:200` tells the model \"Errors go to **stderr** as one line of JSON in the same envelope\", and `cmd/talaria/root.go:138-140` states the rule explicitly — \"stderr carries one JSON object, and prose in front of it is what an agent parsing stderr would choke on\". An agent doing `json.loads(stderr)` fails. **Fix:** emit warnings inside the versioned envelope (`{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"warning\":{…}}`), or state in AGENT.md that stderr is newline-delimited JSON and make the warnings JSON too.\n\n4. **README claims a scaffolded command tree that does not exist** — WARN\n   `README.md:8` — \"the rest of the command tree is scaffolded.\" `talaria twin` returns `{\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"unknown command \\\"twin\\\" for \\\"talaria\\\"\"}}`; there is no `internal/twin` package and no `twin` command registered in `cmd/talaria/root.go:92-100`. An agent reading the README will try `talaria twin serve` and get a usage error. (Phases 5–8 being unbuilt is in scope; the README asserting otherwise is not.) **Fix:** \"Phases 5–8 (`twin serve`/`record`/`fault`) are designed but not implemented.\"\n\n5. **\"Every executed call carries a `validation` block\" is false for `history replay`** — WARN\n   `cmd/talaria/history.go:225` passes `nil` for the validation result (`callPayload(req, …, nil)`); the comment at `:223` explains replay has no spec to check against. But `README.md:333` and `AGENT.md:81` both say *every* executed call carries the block, and replay is an executed call that emits the same envelope minus `validation`. An agent that unconditionally reads `validation.body_valid` after a replay hits a missing key. **Fix:** one clause in both docs — \"except `history replay`, which re-sends a recorded request without a spec\".\n\n6. **Missing-path-parameter error contradicts itself, and the text propagates into `run` reports and JUnit XML** — WARN\n   `internal/request/build.go:244`. When a *declared, required* path param is unbound, both failures are reported:\n   ```\n   --param petId is required (path parameter); path \"/pets/{petId}\" has a placeholder the operation declares no parameter for\n   ```\n   The second clause is wrong — the operation does declare it. Verified verbatim in `call` stderr, in `run --report json` (`\"reason\": …`) and in `<skipped message=\"…\">` in the JUnit output. §3.1 requires errors that say \"what failed, why\". **Fix:** in `binder.path`, only emit the leftover-placeholder error for placeholders with no matching declared param; suppress it when a required-param error was already recorded for that name.\n\n7. **`run --tag <unknown>` exits 2 without `valid_alternatives`, unlike every other unknown-name error** — WARN\n   `cmd/talaria/run.go:217` — `clierr.Usage(\"no operation matches %s\", …)` with no `.WithAlternatives(...)`. `run --operation bogus` correctly lists all five operation ids; `describe`/`uses`/`--output`/`--kind` all list alternatives. §4's exit-2 row requires \"stderr JSON lists valid options\", and the spec's tag set is enumerable at that point. **Fix:** collect the distinct tags (and ids) from the loaded doc and attach them via `WithAlternatives`.\n\n8. **42 MB of agent transcripts and stale review artifacts are committed at repo root** — WARN\n   `.ralph/ralph-20260802-154350.log` (22.8 MB) and `.ralph/ralph-20260802-195025.log` (19.6 MB) account for ~403,000 of the diff's 433,282 inserted lines. `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md` (264 lines) and `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md:1` (\"talaria — Review Fix Plan\", scoped to a completed round of findings) are process artifacts, not deliverables — and `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md:4` points at `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md`, which is deleted in the working tree. For a project whose premise is that transcripts are a leak channel (§1: \"It is in the context window, in the transcript, in the logs\"), shipping full agent transcripts in the repo is also the wrong signal. **Fix:** add `.ralph/*.log` to `.gitignore` and drop the two logs from history; move or delete `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md` and `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` before the PR merges.\n\n9. **`request.headers` value differs from the §4 output sketch** — INFO\n   `internal/request/request.go:101` returns `v.Prefix() + v.ref.String()`, producing `\"Authorization\": \"Bearer <redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>\"`. `docs/design/DESIGN.md:233` shows `\"Authorization\": \"<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>\"` (no scheme prefix). The shipped form is strictly more informative and leaks nothing; §5a:339 only requires \"rendered `<redacted:env:NAME>`\". **Fix:** update the sketch at `DESIGN.md:233` so the doc and the envelope agree byte-for-byte.\n\n10. **`request.headers` and `response.headers` have different shapes in the same envelope** — INFO\n    Request headers serialise as `map[string]string`; response headers as `map[string][]string` (`internal/curl/exec.go:27`, `Headers http.Header`). An agent parsing \"the headers of this envelope\" needs two code paths. **Fix:** document the asymmetry in `AGENT.md` under \"The validation block\"/response, or normalise response headers to single strings joined on `, `.\n\n11. **`--output tsv` falls back to pretty text on `describe`, `call`, `history show` and `version`** — INFO\n    `README.md:477` — \"`tsv` prints bare tab-separated rows with no header line, for `cut` and `awk`\" — is stated for every command. Verified: `describe … --output tsv` and `call … --output tsv` emit the identical multi-line pretty rendering, with no tabs. DESIGN only requires tsv on `list` (`DESIGN.md:167`), so this is doc scope, not a missing feature. **Fix:** qualify README:471-477 — \"`tsv` is a row format; commands with no row shape (`describe`, `call`, `version`) render their pretty form under it.\"\n\n12. **§5 names viper for profile/env layering; it is not a dependency** — INFO\n    `docs/design/DESIGN.md:279` — \"spf13/cobra (+ viper for profile/env layering)\". `go.mod` has no viper; layering is hand-rolled in `internal/config/config.go` over `gopkg.in/yaml.v3`. The result is simpler and does everything §5 Auth requires, but the doc still prescribes viper and `.ralph/stack.json:3` still records \"cobra CLI (spf13/cobra + viper)\". **Fix:** amend `DESIGN.md:279` and `stack.json` to record the decision, as this branch did for the libopenapi-validator strictness question.\n\n13. **`history replay` silently ignores the global `--base-url`** — INFO\n    `cmd/talaria/history.go:567` rebuilds `BaseURL` from the recorded URL unconditionally; `--base-url` is registered persistently at `cmd/talaria/root.go:73`, so it parses and is discarded. Replaying a recorded staging call against localhost is the natural thing to try. The help text says \"to the URL it went to\", so the behaviour is discoverable, but a flag that accepts a value and does nothing is a trap. **Fix:** either honour `--base-url` on replay (re-pointing the recorded path/query at a new host) or reject it with a usage error naming why.",
