  "output_file": "/tmp/claude-1000/-home-casper-projects-talaria/644e759b-1072-45da-9327-14bdce6dc351/tasks/a9f2c5493ec5f275b.output",
  "summary": "Scratch server stopped; repo is unmodified (`git status` matches the pre-review snapshot).\n\n## Integration review — `ralph/design`\n\nI built `/tmp/talaria` from HEAD and drove it against a scratch HTTP echo server (`/tmp/tal/echo.py`) plus the repo's own testdata specs, checking each seam end to end. Full suite (`go test ./...`) is green.\n\n**Verified working** (no findings): Request→curl field fidelity (path params, query order/encoding, repeated query params, cookies, headers, body + media type, bearer/basic/apiKey in all three locations); Swagger 2.0 → 3.x conversion carrying security schemes, `body`/`formData` params and `consumes`/`produces` through to the operation model, the request, and the validator (`internal/spec/testdata/swagger2-apikey.json` and `internal/e2e/testdata/e2e-api-2.0.json` both drive real calls); redaction round-trip — `<redacted:env:NAME>` survives corpus write, `history show` and is re-resolved from the environment by `history replay` (confirmed for header apiKey, query apiKey and `-u` basic); entry ids survive a trim (forced a 1001-entry trim, all ids intact); `show <n>` and `replay <n>` address the same entry; examples → fixtures → gen priority chain reaching the wire; `run` counts/JUnit/exit codes (4 and 5); validation reaching the `validation` block and the exit code; `internal/e2e` and `internal/canary` drive the **real** binary and **real** curl with no fakes; CI runs exactly `.ralph/stack.json`'s commands.\n\n---\n\n### Findings\n\n**1. CRIT — the emitted/`--dry-run` curl sends POST where talaria sends GET**\n`internal/curl/render.go:45`\n\n`Render` omits `-X` for GET, but `bodyArgs` still appends `--data-raw`. curl's own rule is that `-d/--data-raw` without `-X` implies POST. The config document (`internal/curl/config.go:149`) always writes `request = \"GET\"`, so the real call is a GET with a body while the command talaria prints is a POST. This is the same class 2d92e67 fixed for HEAD, left open for GET.\n\nFailure scenario, reproduced:\n```\n$ talaria call api.yaml getEcho --body '{\"a\":1}'\n  request.curl: curl -q -s --data-raw '{\"a\":1}' 'http://127.0.0.1:8791/echo'\n  server saw:   GET  /echo  body={\"a\":1}\n$ <paste that exact command>\n  server saw:   POST /echo  body={\"a\":1}  Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n```\nAn agent that pastes the `curl` field into a bug report, or a human reproducing a 4xx, exercises a different method and a Content-Type talaria never sent — and for a mutation-gated API a \"read-only\" GET reproduction silently becomes a write.\n\nFix: emit `-X` whenever the command carries a body, i.e. change the GET guard to `case req.Method != \"\" && !(strings.EqualFold(req.Method, http.MethodGet) && req.Body == nil):` — or unconditionally `args = append(args, \"-X\", req.Method)` when `req.Body != nil`.\n\n---\n\n**2. CRIT — `auth check` and `call`/`run` disagree about alternative security requirements; exit 5 on a call `auth check` just blessed**\n`internal/config/auth.go:94`\n\n`Resolve` returns on the first requirement whose schemes are of a *supported type*, without consulting `Present()`. `cmd/talaria/auth.go:169` (`satisfied`) implements the opposite, correct rule: any one alternative being present is enough. The two components answer the same question differently, and `auth check` is the documented pre-flight (\"The source follows the same resolution `call` uses\", README:243).\n\nFailure scenario, reproduced with a spec declaring `security: [{keyA: []}, {keyB: []}]` and only `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_KEYB` exported:\n```\n$ talaria auth check api4.yaml     → exit 0  (keyA missing, keyB present)\n$ talaria call api4.yaml getEcho   → exit 5  \"no credential in $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_KEYA: set it and retry\"\n$ talaria run  api4.yaml           → exit 5, every operation skipped\n```\nWorse, `--dry-run` exits 0 and renders `-H \"X-A: $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_KEYA\"`, so the preview points at the wrong scheme too. An agent following AGENT.md's loop asks a human to export a variable the spec did not require.\n\nFix: in `Resolve`'s loop, prefer the first requirement whose credentials are all `Present()`; fall back to the first supported one only if none is fully satisfiable (so the exit-5 message still names something actionable). Reuse `cmd/talaria/auth.go`'s `satisfied` logic rather than duplicating it — the two rules living in two packages is what let them drift.\n\n---\n\n**3. WARN — OpenAPI server variables are dropped between the spec model and the request builder**\n`internal/request/build.go:178`\n\n`firstServer` returns `Servers[0].URL` verbatim and ignores `Servers[0].Variables`, whose `default` values are exactly what makes a templated server usable. `baseURL()` then rejects the templated URL at `build.go:160`.\n\nFailure scenario, reproduced with the very common shape `servers: [{url: \"https://{env}.example.com/{basePath}\", variables: {env: {default: api}, basePath: {default: v1}}}]`:\n```\n$ talaria call api5.yaml getEcho --dry-run\n{\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"cannot build a request for getEcho: base URL \\\"https://{env}.example.com/{basePath}\\\" from the spec's servers[0].url is not an absolute http(s) URL\"}}\n```\nEvery `call` and every operation of `run` fails, and the message never mentions `--base-url`, which is the escape hatch. The `{host}` form is standard OpenAPI, not a malformed spec.\n\nFix: substitute `{name}` → `Variables[name].Default` in `firstServer` before returning (leaving any unsubstituted placeholder to fail as today), and add `; pass --base-url to override` to the `build.go:160` message when the candidate came from the spec.\n\n---\n\n**4. WARN — the `--dry-run == real` e2e assertion cannot detect render/config divergence**\n`internal/e2e/e2e_test.go:454`\n\nThe package doc claims the suite proves \"the curl `--dry-run` shows is the curl `call` runs\", but the assertion compares `dry.Request.Curl` to `called.Request.Curl` — both produced by the *same* `curl.Render` call on the same `*request.Request`. It is a tautology; it can never fail. Nothing in the module executes a rendered command and compares it to what talaria actually sent (`internal/curl/config_test.go:479` runs curl from the config document only). This is precisely why finding 1 survived three review cycles.\n\nFix: add an e2e case that takes `request.curl` from `--dry-run`, runs it through `sh -c` against the same `httptest` server, and asserts the recorded method, path, headers and body match those of the real `call`. Parameterise it over at least GET-with-body, HEAD, a cookie param and a form body.\n\n---\n\n**5. INFO — the request body's media type is absent from `call`'s structured output but present in the corpus**\n`cmd/talaria/call.go:45`\n\n`requestView.Body` is a bare string; the media type appears only inside the rendered `curl` string. `corpus.Body` records `content_type`, so `history show --output json` exposes it and `call --output json` does not. An agent that wants to know what media type its generated body will be sent under has to regex the `curl` field.\n\nFailure scenario: `talaria call swagger2-apikey.json createPet --body '{...}' --dry-run --output json` returns `\"headers\":{\"X-API-Key\":\"…\"}` with no `Content-Type`, while the wire and history both carry `application/json`.\n\nFix: add `BodyContentType string \\`json:\"body_content_type,omitempty\"\\`` to `requestView` and populate it from `req.Body.ContentType` in `callPayload` (call.go:464).\n\n---\n\n**6. INFO — garbled missing-credential message in `run`, inconsistent with `auth check`**\n`cmd/talaria/run.go:342` and `cmd/talaria/run.go:405`\n\nBoth interpolate `pluralise(len(missing), \"scheme\")` into a sentence that already reads \"for security\", producing `no credential for security 1 scheme tok (set $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER)`. `cmd/talaria/auth.go:155` phrases the same condition as `no credential for security scheme tok (…)`. The run.go comment claims it uses \"the same phrasing `auth check` uses\". Already listed on the branch's own outstanding list (`.ralph/pr_body.md:93`), still present.\n\nFix: drop the count — `\"no credential for security scheme%s %s\"` with `\"\"`/`\"s\"` — or call the same helper `auth.go` uses.\n\n---\n\nNo further CRIT findings. The credential firewall itself held across every path I drove: no resolved credential appeared in stdout, stderr, the emitted curl, or `history.jsonl` in any of the ~40 invocations, including the error paths (rejected flags, unparseable base URLs, exit-5 refusals).",
