rnal/spec/convert.go): do converted specs carry security schemes, body params, and consumes/produces through to the operation model and on to the request? Test with internal/spec/testdata/swagger2-apikey.json and internal/e2e/testdata/e2e-api-2.0.json.\n- `run` mode: does the examples -> fixtures -> gen test-data priority chain actually feed the request builder, and do per-operation failures land in the JSON and JUnit reports correctly (counts, failure messages, exit code)?\n- Validation: does internal/validate receive the real response bytes and content type, and does its verdict reach the `validation` block in the output envelope and the exit code?\n- Environment parity: does anything behave differently under test than at runtime? Look for test-only hooks, injected fakes that bypass real code paths, TTY detection, env-var lookups stubbed in tests, or a fake curl used in tests that hides a real bug. `internal/e2e/e2e_test.go` and `internal/canary/canary_test.go` are the integration suites — check whether they exercise the real binary and real curl or a substitute.\n- Are the CI workflow (.github/workflows/ci.yml) checks the same as what a developer runs locally?\n\nBuild and drive the real binary to verify: `go build -o /tmp/talaria ./cmd/talaria` then exercise it against testdata specs and, where an HTTP endpoint is needed, a scratch server you write under /tmp. DO NOT modify any file in the repo.\n\nReport only issues introduced by this branch, each with an exact `file:line`. For each: title, severity (CRIT = data does not reach the next component / broken functionality / wrong result; WARN = fragile seam, missing coverage of a real path, environment divergence; INFO = minor), description with a concrete failure scenario, and a concrete suggested fix.\n\nReturn findings as a numbered list. If you found nothing at a severity, say so explicitly.",
