        "content": "94\t### Task 3: Make `auth check` and `call`/`run` agree on which requirement is chosen\n95\t\n96\t**Fixes findings:** #3\n97\t\n98\t**Files:**\n99\t- `internal/config/auth.go` (modify)\n100\t- `cmd/talaria/auth.go` (modify)\n101\t- `internal/config/auth_test.go` (modify)\n102\t\n103\t**Steps:**\n104\t1. In `internal/config/auth.go`, export one predicate over a security requirement: given the\n105\t   requirement, the declared schemes and the profile, report whether talaria can supply it\n106\t   (`known`) and whether every credential is `Present()`. This is the rule `satisfied`\n107\t   (`cmd/talaria/auth.go:169`) already implements correctly.\n108\t2. Rewrite `Resolve` (:80) to make two passes over `op.Security`: return the first requirement\n109\t   that is both supported and fully present; if none is, fall back to the first supported\n110\t   requirement so the exit-5 message still names an actionable variable. Preserve the current\n111\t   early return for an empty requirement and the \"no usable security scheme\" error when nothing\n112\t   is supported.\n113\t3. Rewrite `satisfied` in `cmd/talaria/auth.go` to call the shared predicate instead of\n114\t   re-deriving it. The duplication across two packages is what let the two answers drift.\n115\t4. Add a test for the reproducing case: `security: [{keyA: []}, {keyB: []}]` with only\n116\t   `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_KEYB` set resolves to `keyB`, and `Resolve` with neither set still\n117\t   returns the `keyA` credential so the error names a variable.\n118\t\n119\t**Verify:** `go test ./...`\n120\t- [ ] With only the second alternative's credential exported, `Resolve` returns it and `call`\n121\t      succeeds where it previously exited 5.\n122\t- [ ] `auth check` and `Resolve` agree on every alternative arrangement the tests cover.\n123\t- [ ] With no credential set at all, the exit-5 message still names a concrete variable.\n124\t"
      }
