        "type": "text",
        "text": "Task 3 done — committed as `33ec554`. Stopping here per the one-task rule.\n\n**What changed**\n\n- `internal/config/auth.go` — added `Coverage` (`Unsupported`/`Incomplete`/`Satisfied`/`Optional`) and `Covers(req, byName)`, the single rule for whether an alternative is usable. `Resolve` now builds the credentials for every supported scheme the operation names, returns the first *covered* requirement, and falls back to the first supported one so the exit-5 error still names a variable.\n- `cmd/talaria/auth.go` — `satisfied` calls `config.Covers` instead of re-deriving the rule in a second package, which is what let the two drift.\n- `README.md:167` — the alternative-selection sentence now describes the present-first rule.\n\n**Verification**\n\nRed first: with only `$TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_KEYB` set, `auth check` exited 0 while `call --dry-run` rendered `-H \"X-A: $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_KEYA\"` — the finding reproduced. After the fix, `go test ./...` is green across all 15 packages, and `gofmt -l` + `go vet ./...` are clean.\n\nNew tests: `TestResolvePrefersTheAlternativeWhoseCredentialIsSet`, `TestResolveFallsBackToTheFirstSupportedAlternativeWhenNoneIsSet`, a four-arrangement `TestResolveAgreesWithTheCoverageAuthCheckReports` table, and a cmd-level `TestAuthCheckAndCallPickTheSameAlternative` that runs the real pre-flight and dry run against a new `alternatives.yaml` fixture.\n\nOne thing I left alone as out of scope: when *neither* alternative has a credential, `unsatisfied`'s message still lists both schemes (\"set $A, $B\") even though only one is needed. It names actionable variables and both are genuine candidates, so it isn't wrong — but an agent reading it may export more than the spec requires. Worth a follow-up finding if you want it tightened."
      }
    ],
