        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "127\t### Task 4: Stop `run` from fabricating failures and history when cancelled\n128\t\n129\t**Fixes findings:** #4\n130\t\n131\t**Files:**\n132\t- `cmd/talaria/run.go` (modify)\n133\t- `cmd/talaria/run_test.go` (modify)\n134\t\n135\t**Steps:**\n136\t1. In the suite loop (`cmd/talaria/run.go:123`), break when the runner's context is cancelled so\n137\t   only operations that actually ran appear in the report. `runner.ctx` is already held (:245,\n138\t   set at :302); the loop never consults it.\n139\t2. In `runner.execute` (:320), when `curl.ExecuteWith` (:371) fails because the context was\n140\t   cancelled, skip `recordCall` (:374) — history must not gain entries for requests that were\n141\t   never made. Those entries land under `SourceRun` and are subject to `trim`'s 1000-per-source\n142\t   cap (`internal/corpus/store.go:31`), so a cancelled run over a large spec evicts the previous\n143\t   run's real history.\n144\t3. In `verdict` (:403), return a distinct error when the context was cancelled, ahead of the\n145\t   `failOnError` branch, so a cancelled run neither exits 0 nor reports a validation verdict for\n146\t   a suite that never ran. Use a non-zero exit distinct from 4 (validation) and 5 (credentials).\n147\t4. Add a test that cancels the runner's context partway through a multi-operation suite and\n148\t   asserts: the report contains only the operations that ran, no cancelled operation is counted\n149\t   as `failed`, no history entry was appended for them, and the exit is neither 0 nor the\n150\t   validation code.\n151\t\n152\t**Verify:** `go test ./...`\n153\t- [ ] A run cancelled after N of M operations reports N results, not M.\n154\t- [ ] `--fail-on-error` on a cancelled run does not produce a validation failure verdict.\n155\t- [ ] The corpus gains no entry for an operation whose request was cancelled before it ran.\n156\t"
      }
    ]
  },
