        "content": "155\t- [ ] The corpus gains no entry for an operation whose request was cancelled before it ran.\n156\t\n157\t---\n158\t\n159\t### Task 5: Scrub sensitive literals, not just resolved credentials, from curl's stderr\n160\t\n161\t**Fixes findings:** #5\n162\t\n163\t**Files:**\n164\t- `internal/curl/exec.go` (modify)\n165\t- `internal/curl/exec_test.go` (modify)\n166\t\n167\t**Steps:**\n168\t1. In `scrubber` (`internal/curl/exec.go:269`), iterate on `p.Value.IsSensitive()` rather than\n169\t   `p.Value.IsSecret()`. A literal typed under a credential-shaped name — `--query api_key=…`,\n170\t   marked `Sensitive` by `hide` (`internal/request/build.go:309`) and rendered `<redacted>`\n171\t   everywhere else — is skipped today, so curl's own error text carries it in cleartext into the\n172\t   exit-1 JSON on stderr.\n173\t2. Split the two cases inside the loop: for a secret keep the current `Ref().Resolve()` →\n174\t   `Ref().String()` mapping; for a sensitive literal use `p.Value.Reveal()` as the needle and\n175\t   `secret.Placeholder` as the replacement. Skip an empty needle either way.\n176\t3. Keep the `url.QueryEscape` companion pair for both branches — a value echoed back inside a\n177\t   URL is percent-encoded, which the literal form walks straight past.\n178\t4. Add a canary test for a literal `--query api_key=` whose call provokes a curl error that\n179\t   echoes the URL (a spec path containing `[` forces curl exit 3), asserting the emitted error\n180\t   text contains the placeholder and not the literal.\n181\t\n182\t**Verify:** `go test ./...`\n183\t- [ ] A curl error echoing a URL that contains a literal `api_key=` value emits the placeholder\n184\t      on stderr, not the value.\n185\t- [ ] The existing secret-ref scrubbing behaviour is unchanged.\n186\t\n187\t---\n188\t\n189\t### Task 6: Restrict `history replay` to credentials inside the auth namespace"
