        "content": "185\t- [x] The existing secret-ref scrubbing behaviour is unchanged.\n186\t\n187\t---\n188\t\n189\t### Task 6: Restrict `history replay` to credentials inside the auth namespace\n190\t\n191\t**Fixes findings:** #6\n192\t\n193\t**Files:**\n194\t- `cmd/talaria/history.go` (modify)\n195\t- `cmd/talaria/history_test.go` (modify)\n196\t\n197\t**Steps:**\n198\t1. In `replayValue` (`cmd/talaria/history.go:655`), stop accepting an arbitrary ref name from\n199\t   the store. `secret.ParseRef` accepts any non-empty name, and both the name and the\n200\t   destination URL come from a plain JSONL file — which makes the binary a \"resolve $ANY_VAR and\n201\t   send it to $ANY_URL\" primitive, run from the one process the deployment trusts with\n202\t   credentials.\n203\t2. Add an allowlist predicate: accept a ref whose name is `config.EnvBearer` (auth.go:21),\n204\t   `config.EnvBasic` (:23), or carries the `config.EnvAPIKeyPrefix` prefix (:26), or is a value\n205\t   in the selected profile's `Auth` map (`internal/config/config.go:67`). Thread the profile\n206\t   into `replayValue` — its caller already resolves one.\n207\t3. Reject anything else with a usage error (exit 2) naming the variable it refused, so the\n208\t   failure is diagnosable. Do not fall through to `request.Literal(stored)`: that would put the\n209\t   literal string `<redacted:env:NAME>` on the wire.\n210\t4. Add tests: a stored `<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>` still replays; a stored\n211\t   `<redacted:env:AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY>` is refused with the name in the message; a name\n212\t   present in the profile's `auth:` map is accepted.\n213\t\n214\t**Verify:** `go test ./...`\n215\t- [ ] A hand-written history entry naming an env var outside the auth namespace is refused with\n216\t      exit 2 rather than resolved and sent.\n217\t- [ ] Replay of an entry recorded by a normal `call` is unaffected.\n218\t- [ ] The refusal message names the variable it refused."
      }
