          "description": "Security review of branch",
          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": true,
          "prompt": "You are the **security** reviewer for a code review of the branch `ralph/design` against `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria. This is a greenfield Go CLI (\"talaria\") — the entire codebase is new on this branch, so the whole of `cmd/` and `internal/` is in scope.\n\nDO NOT fix anything. DO NOT edit any file. Produce findings only.\n\n## Context you must read first\n- `docs/design/DESIGN.md` — the spec. §5a \"The credential firewall\" is THE core requirement: \"no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value.\" Read §3 principle 0, §5, §5a in full.\n- `AGENT.md` and `README.md` — documented behaviour/conventions.\n- `git show HEAD:REVIEW_FINDINGS.md` — TWO prior review rounds already happened. All 6 CRIT findings from the last round were fixed in commits 795fc18..383eb98. **Do not re-report findings already listed there unless you verify the fix is incomplete or wrong** — a genuinely incomplete fix IS a valid new finding, and worth checking carefully. Prior WARN/INFO findings that were never fixed also should NOT be re-reported.\n\n## Scope\nFull branch diff: `git diff main...HEAD`. Ignore `.ralph/*.log` (200k-line agent logs, not reviewable code). Focus on Go source in `cmd/talaria/` and `internal/`.\n\n## Your checklist\n- **Credential firewall (highest priority)**: can any secret value reach stdout, stderr, history (`internal/corpus`), JSON/pretty/tsv output, dry-run, emitted curl, error messages, JUnit reports, or debug output? Trace `internal/secret/`, `internal/curl/exec.go`, `internal/curl/config.go`, `internal/curl/render.go`, `internal/request/build.go`, `cmd/talaria/history.go`.\n- Injection: shell/argv construction, curl config-document escaping (`escapeDirective`), header/CRLF injection, URL construction.\n- Path traversal: spec loading (`internal/spec/source.go`), fixtures (`internal/gen/fixtures.go`), corpus paths, `--body @file`, temp files. Are paths validated? Symlinks resolved? Temp file permissions (0600) and cleanup?\n- Secrets committed in the diff (keys, tokens) — check testdata too.\n- File permissions on config (`~/.config/talaria/config.yaml` should be 0600), history store, corpus files.\n- TOCTOU / race on the corpus lock (`internal/corpus/lock_unix.go`).\n- Redaction bypass: any path that reconstructs a raw value from a redacted one.\n- Untrusted input from the OpenAPI spec itself (a spec is untrusted per §1: \"point it at any API's OpenAPI doc\").\n- Subprocess handling: `internal/curl/exec.go`, `procgroup_unix.go` — env inheritance, argv exposure, orphaned processes.\n\n## Verification bar\nEvery finding must be REAL and REPRODUCIBLE. Where practical, actually prove it: build the binary (`go build ./...` works, `go build -o /tmp/talaria-review ./cmd/talaria`), write a scratch spec in /tmp, run it, use a local listener (`nc -l` or a tiny Go/python http server on 127.0.0.1) to observe the wire. Speculation is worthless here — the previous rounds had reproductions and that is the standard. If you cannot reproduce it, either drop it or mark it clearly as unverified and downgrade the severity.\n\nDo not create or modify any file inside the repo. Use /tmp for scratch work.\n\n## Output\nReturn a list of findings in exactly this shape, most severe first:\n\n```\n## <Title>\n- **Severity:** CRIT | WARN | INFO\n- **File:** path/to/file.go:LINE   (must be a real, current line number)\n- **Description:** what is wrong, why it matters, and the reproduction (commands + observed output).\n- **Suggested fix:** concrete.\n```\n\nSeverity rules: CRIT = exploitable vulnerability, data loss, broken functionality, or an unmet DESIGN.md requirement. WARN = missing validation, hardening gap, architectural concern. INFO = style/docs. Be honest — inflating a WARN to CRIT wastes a fix cycle; downgrading a real bug ships it. Report at most ~10 findings; quality over quantity. If you find nothing new, say so plainly."
        },
