  "prompt": "You are the **concurrency** reviewer for a code review of the branch `ralph/design` against `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria. This is a greenfield Go CLI (\"talaria\") — the entire codebase is new on this branch, so all of `cmd/` and `internal/` is in scope.\n\nDO NOT fix anything. DO NOT edit any file. Produce findings only.\n\n## Context\n- `docs/design/DESIGN.md` — the spec (esp. §4 exit codes, §5a on the curl subprocess and stdin ownership, §4 `--timeout` semantics: \"seconds, per operation; one wedged endpoint never costs the report\").\n- `git show HEAD:REVIEW_FINDINGS.md` — two prior review rounds. Prior CRITs (including \"Ctrl-C during run fabricates failures\", fixed in 64a3504, and \"cancel curl on a signal instead of orphaning it\", 94a68c3, and \"serialise history appends so trim cannot destroy entries\", e0cceae) are already fixed. **Do not re-report those unless you verify the fix is incomplete or introduced a new bug** — checking whether those specific fixes are correct and complete is one of the most valuable things you can do.\n\n## Scope\n`git diff main...HEAD` (ignore `.ralph/*.log`). The concurrency-relevant surfaces:\n- `internal/curl/exec.go`, `internal/curl/procgroup_unix.go`, `internal/curl/procgroup_other.go`, `internal/curl/sweep.go` — subprocess lifecycle, stdin/stdout/stderr pipes, timeouts, context cancellation, signal handling, process-group kill.\n- `cmd/talaria/run.go` — the suite loop, cancellation, per-operation timeout.\n- `cmd/talaria/root.go` — signal context setup.\n- `internal/corpus/store.go`, `internal/corpus/lock_unix.go`, `internal/corpus/lock_other.go`, `internal/corpus/entry.go` — file locking, concurrent append, trim, atomic rename.\n- `cmd/talaria/history.go` — read/write interleaving.\n\n## Your checklist\n- **Data races** on shared state; unsynchronized map/slice access. (`go test -race ./...` currently passes — so look for races the tests do not exercise.)\n- **Goroutine leaks**: is every spawned goroutine guaranteed to exit? Look for goroutines writing to unbuffered channels whose reader may have returned (timeout/cancel paths are the classic).\n- **Subprocess leaks**: is curl always reaped? `cmd.Wait` always called? Pipes always closed? What happens if writing the config document to curl's stdin blocks because curl exited early (EPIPE / deadlock)?\n- **Deadlocks**: writing to a subprocess stdin pipe while not draining its stdout/stderr can deadlock on a large body or large response. Check whether stdout/stderr are drained concurrently with the stdin write. Test with a large body (e.g. 10MB) and a large response.\n- **Context cancellation honoured** everywhere: does the timeout actually fire? Does SIGINT/SIGTERM propagate and terminate curl and its process group? Does a second Ctrl-C work?\n- **Errors swallowed** instead of propagated — ignored return values on Close/Wait/Sync/rename, `_ =` on things that matter.\n- **File locking correctness**: is the corpus lock actually held across the read-modify-write? Is it released on every path including error/panic? Does it work across processes (flock semantics), and what about the same process holding it twice? Is trim safe against a concurrent append from another process?\n- **Atomicity**: is the history store append atomic (partial write on crash / concurrent writer interleaving)? Is trim's rewrite atomic (temp file + rename), and does it preserve entries written after it read?\n\n## Verification bar\nProve it. Build (`go build -o /tmp/talaria-conc ./cmd/talaria`). Write scratch Go tests or shell scripts in /tmp. Run concurrent invocations of the binary against a local slow/hanging HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 to test timeout, cancellation, and concurrent history writes. Check for stray curl processes after a kill (`pgrep -f curl`). Speculation is worthless — the previous rounds reported reproductions with observed output, and that is the bar.\n\nDo not create or modify any file inside the repo. Use /tmp for scratch work.\n\n## Output\nFindings, most severe first, in exactly this shape:\n\n```\n## <Title>\n- **Severity:** CRIT | WARN | INFO\n- **File:** path/to/file.go:LINE   (real current line number)\n- **Description:** what is wrong, why it matters, and the reproduction (commands + observed output).\n- **Suggested fix:** concrete.\n```\n\nSeverity: CRIT = a real hang, deadlock, race, leak, or data loss that a user can hit. WARN = fragile pattern, unhandled error, theoretical race. INFO = style. Be honest — do not inflate. Report at most ~10 findings. If you find nothing new, say so plainly.",
