          "subagent_type": "general-purpose",
          "run_in_background": true,
          "prompt": "You are the **integration** reviewer for a code review of the branch `ralph/design` against `main` in /home/casper/projects/talaria. This is a greenfield Go CLI (\"talaria\") — the entire codebase is new on this branch, so all of `cmd/` and `internal/` is in scope.\n\nDO NOT fix anything. DO NOT edit any file. Produce findings only.\n\n## Context\n- `docs/design/DESIGN.md` — the spec. §4 (CLI surface, output shape, exit codes), §5 (architecture), §5a (credential firewall), §7 (Phases 1–4 are the claimed scope; the twin, Phases 5–8, is NOT in scope).\n- `AGENT.md` — the operating manual for LLM agents; DESIGN.md §3.7 makes it a first-class deliverable. **Every example command in AGENT.md and README.md should actually work as written.** Run them.\n- `git show HEAD:REVIEW_FINDINGS.md` — two prior review rounds; the CRITs listed there are already fixed (commits 795fc18..383eb98 and earlier). Do not re-report them unless a fix is incomplete.\n\n## Scope\n`git diff main...HEAD` (ignore `.ralph/*.log`). You are reviewing whether the *components actually work together end to end*, not individual functions.\n\n## Your checklist — verify, do not assume\n- **Data actually flows between components.** Does what `internal/request` builds reach `internal/curl` intact? Does what curl returns reach `internal/output` and `internal/validate` intact? Does a `call` actually land in `internal/corpus` such that `history show` and `history replay` can reconstruct it? Round-trip everything.\n- **The documented agent loop works**: `list` → `search`/`uses` → `describe` → `call --dry-run` → `call` → `history` → `run`. Run it against a real local HTTP server (start a tiny Go/python server on 127.0.0.1) with a real OpenAPI spec you write in /tmp. Does each step's output actually feed the next (e.g. is an operationId printed by `list` accepted verbatim by `describe` and `call`)?\n- **`history replay`** — does replaying a recorded call actually reproduce the same request on the wire? Compare against a listener. Does it work for POST bodies, headers, query params?\n- **`run`** — do reports (json/junit/pretty) reflect what actually happened? Does `--fail-on-error` exit 4? Does `--timeout` work per-operation? Does the examples→fixtures→gen data chain actually produce the right body?\n- **The emitted curl command actually reproduces the request.** Take `request.curl` from a real `call`, paste it into a shell against a listener, and diff the two requests byte for byte (method, path, query, headers, body). Any divergence is a finding — this is a DESIGN.md §3.4 promise (\"a portable reproduction\"). A prior round found two of these; check the rest (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE, form data, `@file` bodies, cookies, basic auth, query api keys, `--output tsv`).\n- **Environment parity**: does anything behave differently in tests than at runtime? Look for test-only code paths, hardcoded test assumptions, TTY-detection differences, env vars only set in tests. Do the tests actually exercise the real binary path?\n- **Exit codes end to end** (§4 table: 0/1/2/3/4/5) — produce each one for real.\n- **Output modes**: does `--output json|pretty|tsv` work on every command that claims it? Is the JSON valid and jq-parseable on every command and every error path? Is `tsv` structurally sound (no embedded tabs/newlines breaking rows)?\n- **Cross-format specs**: Swagger 2.0 JSON, OpenAPI 3.0 YAML, 3.1 JSON — does the whole loop work on each, or only on the fixture used in tests?\n- **CI workflow** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`): does it actually run what it claims? Would it catch a regression? Does it match `.ralph/stack.json`'s recorded commands?\n\n## Verification bar\nBuild it and run it: `go build -o /tmp/talaria-int ./cmd/talaria`. Write scratch specs and a local server in /tmp. Show observed output in every finding. A prior round's findings included byte-level wire captures — that is the bar. Do not report anything you have not actually run.\n\nDo not create or modify any file inside the repo. Use /tmp for scratch work.\n\n## Output\nFindings, most severe first, in exactly this shape:\n\n```\n## <Title>\n- **Severity:** CRIT | WARN | INFO\n- **File:** path/to/file.go:LINE   (real current line number)\n- **Description:** what breaks, why it matters, and the reproduction (commands + observed output).\n- **Suggested fix:** concrete.\n```\n\nSeverity: CRIT = a broken user-visible workflow, wrong output, or unmet DESIGN.md promise. WARN = rough edge, partial support, inconsistency. INFO = polish. Be honest about severity. Report at most ~12 findings, quality over quantity."
        },
