        "tool_use_id": "toolu_014wpVqvv5Vj3cdjp21yR5eo",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "6:> **Status: early implementation.** `talaria version`, `talaria list`, `talaria describe`,\n7:> `talaria search`, `talaria uses`, `talaria call`, `talaria auth check`,\n8:> `talaria history` and `talaria run` work so far; the rest of the command tree is scaffolded.\n16:**talaria is the trust boundary.** The agent operates on credential *names*; the binary\n39:## Pointing talaria at a spec\n55:talaria list https://petstore3.swagger.io/api/v3/openapi.json\n56:talaria list ./openapi.yaml --tag pets\n71:talaria describe ./openapi.yaml getPet\n72:talaria describe getPet --spec ./openapi.yaml --depth 2\n115:talaria search ./openapi.yaml invoice\n116:talaria search ./openapi.yaml invoice --kind schema\n137:talaria uses ./openapi.yaml Pet\n156:Credentials are named, never shown. talaria maps each security scheme a spec declares onto an\n167:carry it. When a spec offers several alternative security requirements, talaria uses the first\n189:`gopher` and `smb`, and a spec talaria was pointed at is untrusted input.\n194:be one talaria stores in cleartext forever. Use `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC=user:password` instead, which\n201:A literal token in the file is refused, because a credential talaria can read from a config file\n208:can stop talaria redacting an `Authorization` header:\n234:talaria auth check ./openapi.yaml\n235:talaria auth check ./openapi.yaml --profile staging --output json\n254:talaria cannot supply at all (OAuth2, OpenID Connect) are left out rather than reported missing.\n261:talaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42 --query verbose=true\n283:the call talaria made: it stops curl reading `~/.curlrc`, so no directive in a file talaria did\n307:solution: talaria cannot tell a secret field from an ordinary one by looking at it, so an\n311:URL, and URLs are written to server access logs. talaria warns once on stderr when an operation\n330:Interrupting talaria — Ctrl-C, or a `kill` — stops the request rather than leaving it running.\n368:Every call talaria makes is recorded, so an agent can answer \"what have I already tried, and\n372:talaria history\n373:talaria history --operation getPet --since 1h --status 4xx\n374:talaria history show 3\n375:talaria history replay 3\n391:`--allow-mutations`. A header whose value was a literal talaria redacted by name cannot be\n396:map names. That is exactly the set talaria records, so a normal replay is unaffected — but the store\n410:talaria processes can share one history file: each append takes an exclusive advisory lock on\n412:talaria reported as recorded is one you will find in the file.\n424:TALARIA_HISTORY=off talaria call ./openapi.yaml getPet --param petId=42\n438:talaria run ./openapi.yaml --base-url http://localhost:9000\n439:talaria run ./openapi.yaml --tag pets --operation getPet --fail-on-error\n440:talaria run ./openapi.yaml --fixtures ./fixtures --allow-mutations --report json\n474:talaria run ./openapi.yaml --base-url \"$STAGING\" --report junit > report.xml\n487:Every command takes `--output json|pretty|tsv`. With no flag, talaria prints `pretty` when\n510:| 2 | Usage error — unknown operation, a command group named without its subcommand (`talaria auth`), missing required parameter, bad flag value |\n521:go build -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n522:./talaria version\n528:go build -ldflags \"-X main.version=v0.1.0\" -o talaria ./cmd/talaria\n535:command and every output format, and greps every byte talaria emits or writes for the injected\n546:## Driving talaria from an agent\n555:`TALARIA_*` variable against the ones talaria actually reads, so a manual that drifts from the\n556:binary fails the build. It also *runs* the examples: every fenced `talaria …` line is executed",
        "is_error": false
      }
