and the suite *\"gates every release thereafter.\"* `TestErrorPathsDoNotLeakTheCredential` covers seven stages (spec load, operation lookup, parameter binding, mutation gate, body read, curl exec, history index) and opens with a stale exemption:\n\n  ```go\n  // A response-validation failure is not here because response validation is\n  // not built yet (plan tasks 26 and 27). Whoever adds --fail-on-error adds\n  // the case; the stages that exist are all covered.\n  ```\n  Phase 3 and `--fail-on-error` shipped on this branch (`cmd/talaria/call.go`, `run.go:162`), and the case was never added — so the exit-4 envelope, the `validation.errors[].message` (which embeds the request path), and the JUnit `<failure message=…>` built from it are all outside the gate. I probed the path by hand (query API key `CANARY_abcdef123456`, response violating the declared schema, `call --fail-on-error`) and found **no leak** in stdout, stderr or the history store — so this is an unguarded surface rather than a live leak, but it is the one the doc singles out.\n- **Suggested fix:** Delete the comment and add two stages to the table: a `call --fail-on-error` whose response violates the schema (exit 4), and the same through `run --report junit --fail-on-error`, both with a query-string API-key canary set so the validation message's URL is in scope.\n\n## No canary mechanism drives a profile-supplied credential, the second of the two auth sources §5 defines\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/canary/canary_test.go:99 (`mechanisms`)\n- **Description:** DESIGN.md §5a: *\"A CI suite injects canary secrets through **every auth mechanism**…\"* §5 Auth defines two credential sources: env vars by convention, and *\"Profiles (`~/.config/talaria/config.yaml`, mode 0600)… Secrets in profiles may reference env vars.\"* The `mechanisms` table covers five env-var paths (bearer, basic, apikey header/query/cookie) and nothing else. The only profile case in the suite is `TestACredentialResolutionFailureNamesNoValue` (:650), which asserts a *literal* profile entry is **refused** — the supported `${VAR}` form (`profileRef`, internal/config/auth.go:348) is never driven through the format × surface matrix. That path is a distinct resolution branch: it overrides the convention name (`credentialFor`, :302-307) and feeds `history replay`'s allow-list via `ReferencesEnv` (:328). I ran it by hand across json/pretty/tsv/junit plus the history store with `MY_TOKEN=PROFCANARY_…` and found no leak, so again: a gate hole, not a live leak.\n- **Suggested fix:** Add a sixth `mechanism` whose harness writes a 0600 profile with `auth: {bearerAuth: ${CANARY_TOKEN}}` and sets `CANARY_TOKEN`, so it is exercised by `TestNoAuthMechanismLeaksIntoAnyOutputSurface` and `TestNoReportFormatLeaksTheCredential` like the others.\n\n## `run --fail-on-error` exits 0 on a suite where every operation was skipped, contradicting the project's own \"nothing tested is not a pass\" rule\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:450 (`verdict`)\n- **Description:** DESIGN.md §7 Phase 4 calls `run` \"CI-ready\", and README.md:444 / AGENT.md:122 both state the principle: *\"A filter that matches nothing exits **2** rather than passing with nothing tested.\"* `verdict` only consults `view.Summary.Failed`, so a suite in which every operation was skipped is indistinguishable from a suite that passed:\n\n  ```\n  $ talaria run mut.yaml --report json --fail-on-error     # spec is one DELETE + one POST\n  {\"summary\":{\"total\":2,\"passed\":0,\"failed\":0,\"skipped\":2}}   exit=0\n  $ talaria run mut.yaml --tag nosuch --report json           # filter matches nothing\n  {\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"no operation matches --tag \\\"nosuch\\\"\"}}   exit=2\n  ```\n  A mutation-heavy spec run in CI without `--allow-mutations`, or (per the OAuth2 finding above) any spec talaria cannot authenticate, goes green having sent zero requests. The two cases are the same condition — nothing was tested — reported two different ways.\n- **Suggested fix:** In `verdict`, when `failOnError` is set and `Summary.Passed == 0 && Summary.Failed == 0 && Summary.Skipped > 0`, exit nonzero with a message naming the skip reasons; or add an explicit `--fail-on-skip`. Document whichever is chosen in AGENT.md's \"A skip is not a failure\" paragraph, since it currently states the opposite.\n\n## Swagger 2.0 \"conversion fidelity against real 2.0 specs\" is verified only against 3-operation miniatures\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/spec/convert_test.go:87\n- **Description:** DESIGN.md §5, Library choices: *\"Two OpenAPI dependencies is the cost of the 'any API doc' promise — much of the real world is still 2.0… **Phase 1 must verify conversion fidelity against real 2.0 specs.**\"* The suite has six conversion tests over three fixtures of 3 operations each (`petstore-2.0.json`, `swagger2.yaml`, `swagger2-apikey.json`), and they cover only path/query params, `host`+`basePath`+`schemes`, `securityDefinitions` for apiKey/basic, and `#/definitions` refs. The 2.0 constructs that `openapi2conv` actually handles imperfectly are untested: `in: formData` (incl. `type: file`), `in: body`, `collectionFormat`, global `consumes`/`produces`, response `headers`, and `securityDefinitions` of `type: oauth2`. Probing a hand-built 2.0 spec exercising those:\n\n  ```\n  $ talaria describe sw2.json uploadFile --output json\n  …\"request_body\":{\"required\":false,\"content\":[{\"content_type\":\"multipart/form-data\",\n     \"schema\":{…\"properties\":[{\"name\":\"file\",\"type\":\"string\",\"required\":true},…\n  ```\n  Both formData params are `required: true` in the source, yet the converted `requestBody.required` is `false`, and `type: file` lands as a bare `string` with no `format: binary` — so `run`'s mutation gate and generator see a body the API will reject. These are conversion artifacts rather than talaria bugs, which is precisely why the doc asks for them to be *verified* in Phase 1 rather than assumed.\n- **Suggested fix:** Add one adversarial 2.0 fixture carrying formData (incl. `type: file`), a `body` parameter, `collectionFormat: multi`, global `consumes`/`produces`, response `headers` and an oauth2 `securityDefinition`, with assertions pinning what survives — the same shape as the `strict-3.0.yaml` fixture that pins libopenapi-validator's 3.0 behaviour. Where a construct provably does not survive, say so in README's Swagger 2.0 paragraph rather than leaving it silent.\n\n---\n\n**Verified clean** (no finding): the §5 boundary rules (`go list -deps` shows `operation`, `validate`, `gen` import neither `curl` nor `twin`); every §4 flag is present and functional; the `call` JSON shape matches the §4 sketch field-for-field (`schema`, `request.curl/method/url/headers`, `response.status/headers/body/timing_ms`, `validation.status_documented/body_valid/errors`, plus additive `dry_run` and `content_type_documented`); all six exit codes reproduce in the situations §4 describes; pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped; symbolic curl for every mechanism including the query-string key; the query-key stderr warning fires exactly once per invocation including across a multi-operation `run`; curl ≥ 7.70 preflight; `--body` literal/`@file`/`-` with the Go process owning real stdin; the 0600 temp-file fallback; `Set-Cookie` redaction; `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER`/`BASIC`/`APIKEY_<SCHEME>` conventions; 3.0/3.1/3.2 load. `gofmt`, `go vet` and `go test ./...` are all clean at HEAD. The `docs/design/DESIGN.md` diff on this branch is legitimate — it documents `--timeout` (real, implemented), corrects the sketch to the `curl -q` actually emitted, and resolves the §8 libopenapi-validator strictness question with a pinned adversarial fixture; none of it is the spec being bent to excuse the code.",
