  "summary": "Test servers stopped; repo is unmodified (`git status` matches the pre-review state). Findings below — everything was reproduced against a real capture server on 127.0.0.1 with scratch specs in `/tmp/tal`.\n\n---\n\n## Server variables in `servers[].url` are never substituted — `call`/`run` dead-end on a whole class of real 3.x specs\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** internal/request/build.go:158\n- **Description:** OpenAPI 3.x server templating (`{scheme}://host:{port}/base` with `variables:` defaults) is standard and common in real specs. `baseURL()` parses the raw string and rejects anything non-absolute; nothing ever expands the declared defaults. The whole documented loop (`list` → `describe` → `call`) discovers operations fine and then cannot call any of them, and the error blames the spec rather than naming the gap.\n\n  ```\n  $ cat srv.yaml   # servers[0].url: \"{scheme}://127.0.0.1:{port}/base\"\n  $                # variables: scheme{default: http}, port{default: \"9977\"}\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int list ./srv.yaml --output tsv\n  GET\t/pets\tlistPets\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int call ./srv.yaml listPets --dry-run; echo \"rc=$?\"\n  {\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"cannot build a request for listPets:\n   base URL \\\"{scheme}://127.0.0.1:{port}/base\\\" from the spec's servers[0].url is not an\n   absolute http(s) URL\"}}\n  rc=2\n  ```\n  The same code path also contradicts its own doc comment (build.go:132-133: *\"A spec whose server URL is relative … counts as no server\"*). It does not — a relative server hard-fails instead of falling through to the helpful message:\n  ```\n  $ cat srv2.yaml   # servers: [{url: /v1}]\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int call ./srv2.yaml listPets --dry-run\n  {\"…\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"cannot build a request for listPets: base URL \\\"/v1\\\" from\n   the spec's servers[0].url is not an absolute http(s) URL\"}}\n  ```\n  Compare the message the comment intends, which only fires when `servers` is absent entirely: `\"no base URL: the spec declares no server, so pass --base-url or set one in a profile\"`. Neither AGENT.md nor README mentions server variables, so an agent hitting this has no next step. (Swagger 2.0 `basePath` *is* handled correctly — `basePath: /v2` yields `http://127.0.0.1:9977/v2/pets`.)\n- **Suggested fix:** Expand `Server.Variables` defaults in `firstServer` (libopenapi exposes them on `v3high.Server`) before the absoluteness check; a variable with no default and no override stays an error. Separately, make the relative-URL case fall through to the `no base URL … pass --base-url` message the comment promises, and name server variables in AGENT.md's `--base-url` bullet.\n\n---\n\n## `run`'s test-data chain ignores media-type and parameter-level `example`/`examples` — only schema-level `example` is honoured\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** internal/operation/extract.go:166\n- **Description:** DESIGN.md §5 (\"Test data for `run` mode\") and AGENT.md:122 both promise `spec example/examples → fixtures → gen`. `extract.go:166` copies only `media.Schema` off each `MediaType`, dropping `media.Example` and `media.Examples`; `operation.Param` (operation.go:49-60) has no example field at all. So `internal/gen` (fixtures.go:175, fixtures.go:206) can only ever find `schema.example` — the *least* common of the four places an author writes an example. `run` therefore fires random generated data at a real API when the spec has a perfectly good example sitting right there.\n\n  Spec `/tmp/tal/exp.yaml` places an example at each of the four levels; observed wire (`run --allow-mutations`):\n  ```\n  GET /a/860 HTTP/1.1        <- parameter-level `example: 1234`   IGNORED\n  GET /b/5678 HTTP/1.1       <- schema-level  `example: 5678`     used\n  POST /c HTTP/1.1\n  {}                         <- mediaType `example: {k: FROM_MEDIA_EXAMPLE}`  IGNORED\n  POST /d HTTP/1.1\n  {\"k\":\"delta-568\"}          <- mediaType `examples: {one: {value: …}}`       IGNORED\n  POST /e HTTP/1.1\n  {\"k\":\"FROM_SCHEMA_EXAMPLE\"} <- schema-level example              used\n  ```\n  Same on the main fixture: `createPet` declares `example: {id:7,name:\"Rex\",tag:\"cat\"}` on the `application/json` media type; `run` sends `{\"id\":860,\"name\":\"foxtrot-53\",\"tag\":\"delta-391\"}`. It also means `describe` never surfaces those examples to the agent.\n- **Suggested fix:** Add `Example any` / `Examples map[string]any` to `operation.MediaType` and `operation.Param`, populate them in `extractContent`/`extractParams`, and consult them ahead of `exampleValue(schema)` in `gen.paramValue` and `gen.bodyFor`. Precedence within \"examples\": media-type `example` → first `examples` entry's `value` → schema `example`.\n\n---\n\n## `--output tsv` is not structurally sound: variable field counts and rows split by spec text\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** internal/output/render.go:108\n- **Description:** `tsvRenderer.Render` joins cells with `\\t` and no escaping or sanitising, and two callers feed it unsafe cells.\n\n  (a) `cmd/talaria/list.go:103` passes `op.Summary` raw. A tab or a newline in a summary — YAML block scalars are routine — silently rewrites the grid. Two operations produce five lines with 5/1/4/1/0 fields:\n  ```\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int list ./tabs.yaml --output tsv | cat -A\n  GET^I/a^Itabby^Icol1^Icol2 and a$\n  newline here$\n  GET^I/b^Imulti^Ifirst line$\n  second line$\n  $\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int list ./tabs.yaml --output tsv | awk -F'\\t' '{print NR\": \"NF\" fields\"}'\n  1: 5 fields\n  2: 1 fields\n  3: 4 fields\n  4: 1 fields\n  ```\n  An agent doing `list --output tsv | cut -f3` to feed `describe`/`call` — the documented chain — silently gets `col1` as an operationId. `search` already fixes this via `collapse()` (internal/operation/search.go:515), so the inconsistency is visible within the codebase: `search --output tsv` on the same spec correctly emits `…^Icol1 col2 and a newline here`.\n\n  (b) `cmd/talaria/run.go:590` appends a deterministic single-cell summary row to the same table, so `--report tsv` *always* ends with a 1-field row among 6-field rows:\n  ```\n  failed^IGET^I/slow^IgetSlow^I-^Ithe request could not be completed: curl exited 28…$\n  6 operations: 2 passed, 3 failed, 1 skipped$\n  ```\n- **Suggested fix:** Sanitise in `tsvRenderer.Render` (collapse `\\t`, `\\r`, `\\n` in every cell) so no caller can break the format, and drop the summary row from the TSV rendering (keep it for pretty) or pad it to the row width.\n\n---\n\n## JUnit and JSON reports show 0 ms for any operation that timed out or failed to connect\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:415\n- **Description:** `res.TimingMS` is only assigned on the success path; when `ExecuteWith` returns an error the result goes straight to `res.fail(...)` with `TimingMS == nil`, and `runResult.seconds()` (run.go:626) turns that into `0`. A `--timeout 3` operation that consumed the full three seconds is reported as instant, and `<testsuite time>` — which sums the cases — is wrong for the whole suite.\n  ```\n  $ time /tmp/talaria-int run ./api30.yaml --operation getSlow --timeout 3 --report junit\n  <testsuite name=\"talaria run\" tests=\"1\" failures=\"1\" skipped=\"0\" errors=\"0\" time=\"0.000\">\n    <testcase name=\"getSlow\" time=\"0.000\">\n      <failure message=\"…curl: (28) Operation timed out after 3002 milliseconds…\"></failure>\n  real\t0m3.067s\n  ```\n  A CI dashboard tracking suite duration would show a wedged endpoint as free. The `seconds()` doc comment justifies 0 for \"an operation that never reached a server\" — correct for a skip, wrong for a timeout.\n- **Suggested fix:** Time the `curl.ExecuteWith` call in `runner.execute` and set `res.TimingMS` before the `execErr` branch, so a failed operation carries the elapsed time. Leave it nil only for genuine skips.\n\n---\n\n## Warnings on stderr are plain text, breaking the documented \"stderr is one line of JSON\" contract\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/secret/response.go:260\n- **Description:** AGENT.md:206 tells the agent *\"Errors go to **stderr** as one line of JSON in the same envelope\"*, and AGENT.md:194 tells it to *\"Report the warning\"* from a query-string credential. Both land on the same stream unenveloped, so `jq` on stderr fails whenever the two coincide. Same pattern at cmd/talaria/call.go:264 (\"the call was not recorded in history\") and call.go:407 (\"the response was not validated\").\n  ```\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int call ./api30auth.yaml queryKeyedOp --base-url http://127.0.0.1:1 2>err.txt\n  $ cat err.txt\n  warning: this operation sends $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_QKEY in the query parameter \"api_key\"; …\n  {\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":1,\"message\":\"the request could not be completed: …\"}}\n  $ jq . < err.txt\n  jq: parse error: Invalid numeric literal at line 1, column 8\n  ```\n- **Suggested fix:** Emit warnings in the envelope too — `{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"warning\":{\"code\":\"query_credential\",\"message\":\"…\"}}` — one JSON object per line, so stderr is uniformly JSON-lines. Update AGENT.md to say stderr is JSON *lines*, not one line.\n\n---\n\n## `valid_alternatives` is capped at 5, but AGENT.md tells the agent it is the complete set\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/operation/index.go:16\n- **Description:** AGENT.md:212: *\"When `valid_alternatives` is present it is the complete set of right answers. Pick from it rather than guessing again.\"* `maxAlternatives = 5` truncates it, so an agent that follows the instruction literally can never reach an operation outside the top five, and has been told not to guess again.\n  ```\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int list ./api30.yaml --output tsv | wc -l\n  9\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int describe ./api30.yaml nope 2>&1 >/dev/null | jq -c '.error.valid_alternatives'\n  [\"getPet\",\"listPets\",\"patchPet\",\"createPet\",\"deletePet\"]\n  ```\n  `replacePet`, `getBadShape`, `getMissing` and `getSlow` are unreachable from this error. The cap itself is defensible (§3.1's context-dump argument) — the doc is what's wrong, and there is no signal in the payload that the list was truncated.\n- **Suggested fix:** Either add a `\"truncated\": true` / `\"total_alternatives\": 9` sibling field and reword AGENT.md to \"the closest matches, not necessarily the whole set — run `list` if none fit\", or emit the complete set when the spec has few enough operations.\n\n---\n\n## Emitted curl for an inline sensitive header reproduces a *different* request, silently\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/curl/render.go:224\n- **Description:** DESIGN.md §3.4 makes `request.curl` \"a portable reproduction\". For a credential resolved from a `SecretRef` the rendering is correctly symbolic and byte-identical on replay (verified for bearer, basic, apiKey header/query/cookie, and for GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/HEAD/OPTIONS, `@file`, stdin, form and text bodies — all MATCH). But a value the caller supplied inline on a sensitive-named header renders the literal string `<redacted>`, and `word.credential` marks it inert, so the emitted command runs happily and sends different bytes:\n  ```\n  $ /tmp/talaria-int call ./repro2.yaml headOp --header 'Cookie=a=1; b=2'\n  → talaria on the wire:   Cookie: a=1; b=2\n  → emitted request.curl:  curl -q -s -I -H 'Cookie: <redacted>' 'http://127.0.0.1:9977/h'\n  → that command on the wire: Cookie: <redacted>          (exit 0, 200 OK)\n  ```\n  Same for `--header X-Api-Key=…`, `--header Authorization=…`, `--header X-Request-Token=…`. AGENT.md:180 says these are \"not copy-pasteable\", but the failure mode is a request that *succeeds* with wrong data rather than one that visibly refuses — an agent pasting this into a bug report hands someone a reproduction that does not reproduce.\n- **Suggested fix:** Render inline redactions as an obviously-unrunnable placeholder the shell will reject, or prefix the whole rendered command with a `# request carries N redacted value(s); substitute before running` note, and add a `request.curl_complete: false` flag to the JSON so a consumer can branch.\n\n---\n\n## `call --output pretty` — the TTY default — never shows the response body\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/call.go:475\n- **Description:** DESIGN.md §3.1 locks the default to pretty-on-TTY, so this is what a human sees by default. The pretty table is request line, curl, status line, validation line — the response body and headers are deliberately excluded. The inline comment justifies dropping *headers* (`Set-Cookie` in scrollback); nothing justifies dropping the body, which is the reason the call was made.\n  ```\n  $ script -qec \"/tmp/talaria-int call /tmp/tal/api30.yaml getPet --param petId=1\" /dev/null\n  GET http://127.0.0.1:9977/pets/1\n  curl -q -s 'http://127.0.0.1:9977/pets/1'\n  200 OK in 1ms\n  validation: ok\n  ```\n  The server returned `{\"id\": 42, \"name\": \"Fido\", \"tag\": \"dog\"}`; a human has to re-run with `--output json` to see it.\n- **Suggested fix:** Append the redacted response body to the pretty rows (it already goes through `redactResponse`, and `history show --output pretty` already prints exactly this). Truncate at a line budget with a `… (--output json for the rest)` marker if length is the concern.\n\n---\n\n## `--output tsv` silently renders `pretty` on `describe`, `call` and `history show`\n- **Severity:** INFO\n- **File:** internal/output/render.go:105\n- **Description:** AGENT.md:201 says *\"`--output json|pretty|tsv` on every command\"*; DESIGN.md §4 lists only `json|pretty` for `describe`. Commands whose `Table` is a list of pre-rendered single-cell lines produce byte-identical output for both formats, so `tsv` is accepted and quietly means `pretty`:\n  ```\n  $ diff <(talaria describe ./api30.yaml getPet --output tsv) \\\n         <(talaria describe ./api30.yaml getPet --output pretty) && echo IDENTICAL\n  IDENTICAL\n  ```\n  An agent that branches on format gets no signal that its request was not honoured. Same for `call` and `history show`.\n- **Suggested fix:** Either reject `--output tsv` on commands with no row-oriented shape (exit 2, `valid_alternatives: [\"json\",\"pretty\"]`, which is what `--output junit` already does), or narrow AGENT.md:201 to name the commands that actually have a TSV shape (`list`, `search`, `uses`, `history`, `run --report`).\n\n---\n\n## `run`'s missing-credential message is ungrammatical\n- **Severity:** INFO\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/run.go:447\n- **Description:** `\"no credential for security %s %s\"` with `pluralise(n, \"scheme\")` produces a doubled count:\n  ```\n  $ talaria run ./api30auth.yaml --report json\n  {\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"error\":{\"code\":5,\"message\":\"no credential for security 5 schemes\n   bearerAuth (set $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER), petKey (set $TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_PETKEY), …\"}}\n  ```\n  Compare `auth check`, which gets it right: `\"no credential for security schemes basicAuth (set $TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC), cKey (…)\"`. AGENT.md:143 tells the agent to relay this message to a human verbatim.\n- **Suggested fix:** Use the same helper `auth check` uses, or change the format to `\"no credential for %s: %s\"` so it reads \"no credential for 5 security schemes: bearerAuth (…)\".\n\n---\n\n### Verified working (no finding)\n\nWorth recording, since these were the highest-risk items on the list and they hold up: the emitted curl reproduces the request **byte-for-byte on the wire** for GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/HEAD/OPTIONS, GET-with-body, JSON/form/text/multipart bodies, `@file` and stdin bodies, and all five auth mechanisms (bearer, basic, apiKey header/query/cookie) — diffed against a raw-socket capture server in every case. `history replay` reproduces the original request byte-for-byte for POST bodies, headers, query params and every auth scheme, including profile-scoped variables (and correctly refuses an out-of-namespace variable without `--profile`). All six exit codes (0/1/2/3/4/5) were produced for real. Every error path tested (23 of them) emits valid single-line JSON on stderr. The full loop works identically on Swagger 2.0 JSON, OpenAPI 3.0 YAML and 3.1 JSON, including `basePath` and synthesised operationIds, and IDs printed by `list`/`search` are accepted verbatim by `describe`/`call`. No canary secret leaked into any output surface or into the on-disk history store. Chunked, unicode, binary, 500 KB and 204-empty responses all reach `output` and `validate` intact, including repeated headers. TTY and piped modes agree. Spec-over-URL caching serves offline. SIGINT cancels both `call` and `run` cleanly with exit 1 and no orphaned curl. `go vet`, `gofmt` and `go test ./...` all pass; `.github/workflows/ci.yml` matches `.ralph/stack.json` verbatim and pins the same Go version as `go.mod`; `internal/e2e` builds and drives the real binary rather than the in-process command tree. Every fenced example in AGENT.md and README.md runs as written.",
