  "output_file": "/tmp/claude-1000/-home-casper-projects-talaria/2f6d98ca-eae5-40af-9885-4c3f22f4e36c/tasks/a1b29a7d7afb90118.output",
  "summary": "Cleanup done. Here are my findings.\n\n---\n\n## `history` append is O(store size) per call, holds the exclusive cross-process lock the whole time, and loads the store into memory four times over\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** internal/corpus/store.go:99 (also :115, :126, :161, :247, :293)\n- **Description:** `Append` takes the exclusive flock at :109 and then, inside that critical section, reads and JSON-parses the *entire* store twice (`uniqueID`→`storedIDs`'s `os.ReadFile` at :161, then `trim`'s `os.ReadFile` at :250), builds a third full copy in `kept bytes.Buffer` (:278) and writes a fourth out through `replace` (:293). The retention design guarantees this reaches its own worst case and stays there: `maxPerSource = 1000` × 3 sources × two `MaxBody = 64 KiB` bodies ≈ 394 MB, and once a source is *at* the cap every subsequent append is over it, so the full-file rewrite happens on **every single call**, forever. Measured scaling (1000 `call` entries, one response body each, local zero-latency server):\n\n  | body size | store | wall per `call` | peak RSS |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | — (empty) | 0 | 0.05 s | 19 MB |\n  | 4 KB | 4.3 MB | 0.19 s | 36 MB |\n  | 16 KB | 16.6 MB | 0.33 s | 82 MB |\n  | 64 KB | 65.7 MB | 1.02 s | 253 MB |\n\n  At the design's own maximum (394 MB store, both bodies populated) one `talaria call` costs 5.8 s / 1.59 GB RSS, and `talaria run` over **10 operations against 127.0.0.1 with no latency** takes 55 s and peaks at 1.97 GB:\n\n  ```\n  $ ls -l $XDG_STATE_HOME/talaria/history.jsonl\n  -rw------- 1 casper casper 394409904 ... history.jsonl\n  $ time run ten.yaml --output json     -> exit=0 peakRSS=1967MB   real 0m55.204s\n  $ TALARIA_HISTORY=off time run ten.yaml -> exit=0 peakRSS=19MB    real 0m0.220s\n  ```\n\n  Three separate harms. (a) 250x slowdown on the command an agent runs in a loop. (b) ~2 GB RSS on a CI runner is an OOM kill — and the kill lands mid-`trim`, which is finding 4 below. (c) Because it is all inside the flock, it is *cross-process starvation*: a second talaria started while a `run` is appending took **9.1 s** for one call to a zero-latency endpoint, and `SIGINT` sent to it during that wait was ignored for 4+ s and had no effect at all (the process went on to exit 0) — nothing on the `corpus` path consults a context.\n- **Suggested fix:** Stop making every append a whole-file rewrite. (i) Derive the id without reading the store — a timestamp plus a random suffix, or an in-memory `lastID` for the process — so `storedIDs` disappears. (ii) Amortise `trim`: only rewrite when a source is over cap by some slack (e.g. 10%), so the steady state is a pure `O(1)` append. (iii) Stream the rewrite line-by-line (`bufio.Scanner` over the old file into the temp file) instead of `ReadFile` + `bytes.Buffer`, so peak memory is bounded by the largest entry rather than by the store. (iv) Pass the command's context into `Append` and abandon a wait/rewrite when it is done.\n\n## SIGINT and SIGTERM are swallowed for the whole process lifetime, and the curl version preflight is an unbounded, unkillable hang\n- **Severity:** CRIT\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/root.go:207; internal/curl/version.go:42 (called from internal/curl/exec.go:62)\n- **Description:** Prior review reported these as two separate WARNs (19 and 20); neither is fixed, and together they produce a process that no catchable signal can end. `signal.NotifyContext` diverts both signals into a context and never re-raises — `stop()` runs only when `run` returns — so a second Ctrl-C does nothing. `preflight` runs `exec.Command(path, \"--version\").Output()` with no context, no `WaitDelay` and no `Setpgid`, and it runs at exec.go:62 *before* `execCtx` is built at exec.go:82, so neither `--timeout` nor the signal context reaches it. With a `curl` on PATH that wedges on `--version` (a shell wrapper, a binary on a stalled NFS/autofs mount):\n\n  ```\n  $ curl --version wedges;  talaria call --spec spec.yaml getSlow --timeout 2 &\n  ALIVE after SIGINT#1 / ALIVE after SIGINT#2 / ALIVE after SIGTERM\n  1300456 Sl  00:09  /tmp/talaria-conc call --spec ... --timeout 2\n  1300470       sleep 100000        <- preflight's child, orphaned by the SIGKILL\n  ```\n  Only `kill -9` ends it — the one signal that defeats every deferred cleanup this branch added, and that strands a capture directory in TMPDIR for 24 h. A far more reachable instance needs no unusual curl at all, just a spec URL that does not answer (`internal/spec/source.go:117`, bounded but uninterruptible):\n  ```\n  $ talaria list --spec http://127.0.0.1:19702/hang &\n  ALIVE after SIGINT#1 / ALIVE after SIGINT#2 / ALIVE after SIGTERM\n  exit=3 after 30s\n  ```\n  Two Ctrl-Cs and a `kill` ignored for 30 s on an ordinary flaky spec host.\n- **Suggested fix:** Replace `signal.NotifyContext` with an explicit `signal.Notify` handler that cancels the context on the first signal and then calls `signal.Stop`, so a second signal restores default disposition and kills the process. Separately, thread a context into `preflight` (`preflight(ctx, path)`) wrapped in `context.WithTimeout(ctx, 5*time.Second)` with `cmd.WaitDelay = time.Second` and `isolate(cmd)`, so the preflight is bounded and its children die with it.\n\n## The history store is never fsynced, so a crash can lose the whole file rather than one entry\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/corpus/store.go:298 (`replace`), internal/corpus/store.go:213 (`write`)\n- **Description:** There is no `Sync()` anywhere in the package (`grep -rn '\\.Sync()' --include=*.go .` returns nothing outside tests). `replace` writes the trimmed store to a temp file, `Close`s it, and `os.Rename`s it over the real one at :323 without fsyncing either the temp file or the containing directory. `os.Rename` is atomic with respect to *readers*, not with respect to *writeback*: on ext4 `data=ordered` the rename metadata can reach disk while the temp file's data has not, so a power loss or kernel panic in that window leaves `history.jsonl` truncated or zero-filled — up to 3000 entries gone, where the append-only design would have lost at most the last line. The doc comment at :297 states the opposite guarantee (\"a crash mid-trim leaves the old file intact\"), which is true for the rename but not for the data behind it.\n- **Suggested fix:** `tmp.Sync()` before `tmp.Close()` in `replace`, and fsync the directory after `os.Rename`. In `write`, `f.Sync()` before `f.Close()` so an acknowledged append is durable, since `Append` returning nil is documented at :94-95 to mean \"the entry is in the store\".\n\n## A kill during `trim` leaks a full-store-sized temp file in the state directory that nothing ever removes\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/corpus/store.go:301\n- **Description:** `replace` stages the rewrite as `.history-*` in the state directory and relies on `defer os.Remove(tmp.Name())` (:307), which only runs in-process. `curl.SweepStale` (internal/curl/sweep.go:40) is the only cleanup talaria has and it scans TMPDIR for `talaria-call-`/`talaria-body-` prefixes only — it never looks at the state directory. Reproduced by SIGKILLing a `call` 5.3 s into an append against a 394 MB store:\n  ```\n  -rw------- 1 casper casper 101388288 ... /tmp/tconc/state9/talaria/.history-3128772551\n  -rw------- 1 casper casper 392576217 ... /tmp/tconc/state9/talaria/history.jsonl\n  ```\n  101 MB stranded; a later kill at a later point leaks up to the store's full size, and subsequent talaria runs never clean it. This composes badly with finding 1: the ~2 GB RSS makes OOM kills likely, and every OOM kill during trim can leave another few hundred MB behind.\n- **Suggested fix:** Extend the sweep to the state directory — remove `.history-*` entries older than a short grace period (an in-flight trim lasts seconds, not hours) — and call it from the same place `SweepStale` is called (cmd/talaria/root.go:205). Alternatively have `Append` unlink stale `.history-*` siblings while it already holds the lock.\n\n## The process-group kill still has no already-reaped guard\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/curl/procgroup_unix.go:33\n- **Description:** Re-verified: prior finding 18 is unfixed. `syscall.Kill(-cmd.Process.Pid, syscall.SIGKILL)` signals a bare negated pid with no check that the child is still alive. `os/exec`'s `Wait` calls `Process.Wait()` — reaping the child and freeing its pid — and only then receives from `ctxResult`, so `watchCtx` can invoke `Cancel` after the reap (I confirmed the ordering in `$GOROOT/src/os/exec/exec.go:796-880`). If the pid has been recycled as a process-group leader in that window, talaria SIGKILLs an unrelated group owned by the same uid. The stdlib's default `Cancel` is immune because `os.Process` tracks `statusDone` and returns `ErrProcessDone`; overriding it with a raw negated-pid kill discards that. Window is microseconds and pid wrap is slow, so still WARN — but note the error handling *does* work correctly: on ESRCH the fallback `cmd.Process.Kill()` returns `os.ErrProcessDone`, which `watchCtx` explicitly treats as \"don't inject a needless error\", so no spurious failure is reported.\n- **Suggested fix:** Probe with `cmd.Process.Signal(syscall.Signal(0))` first and return its error (os/exec reads `ErrProcessDone` as \"already finished\") before issuing the group kill.\n\n## The non-unix corpus lock is still a silent no-op\n- **Severity:** WARN\n- **File:** internal/corpus/lock_other.go:11\n- **Description:** Re-verified: prior finding 21 is unfixed. `func lock(string) (func(), error) { return func() {}, nil }` under `//go:build !unix` (excludes Windows, js, wasip1). On those platforms two concurrent processes both run the unserialised read-modify-write in `Append` and the loser's entry is erased by the winner's `replace()` rename, with `Append` returning `nil` both times so the loss is never reported — contradicting the contract stated at store.go:94-95. CI is `ubuntu-latest` only, so it stays latent, but DESIGN.md:459 promises per-platform binaries. (For the record, the unix path is correct — see the verification note below.)\n- **Suggested fix:** Implement with `golang.org/x/sys/windows.LockFileEx`, or keep it dependency-free and make it loud: return an error from `lock` on `!unix`, or skip `trim` there so appends stay strictly append-only and nothing can be destroyed.\n\n## `--timeout` above ~9.2e9 seconds silently reverts to the 30 s default\n- **Severity:** INFO\n- **File:** cmd/talaria/call.go:230\n- **Description:** `time.Duration(seconds * float64(time.Second))` overflows int64 for `seconds > ~9.22e9`; on amd64 the out-of-range float→int conversion yields `math.MinInt64`, which `Options.withDefaults` (internal/curl/config.go:54) reads as \"unset\" and replaces with 30 s. Observed: `call --timeout 10000000000` against a never-answering endpoint died after **30 s**, while `--timeout 5` died after 5 s. Nobody types 292 years, so this is cosmetic — but the same conversion means any parse-level nonsense lands on the default silently rather than as a usage error.\n- **Suggested fix:** Reject a `--timeout` that is NaN, infinite, or `> math.MaxInt64/1e9` with a usage error naming the bound, instead of falling through to `withDefaults`.\n\n---\n\n### Verified as correctly and completely fixed (no finding)\n\nI specifically tried to break the three prior CRIT fixes and could not:\n\n- **`64a3504` (Ctrl-C during `run` fabricates failures)** — correct. 30-op suite against a 2 s/request server, SIGINT at t=5 s: report is `{total: 2, passed: 2, failed: 0, skipped: 0}` with only `op0`/`op1` present, history gained exactly 2 lines, stderr `\"the run was cancelled after 2 operations\"`, exit **1**. The in-flight operation is dropped and nothing is fabricated. I also checked the \"curl succeeded exactly as ctx was cancelled\" interleaving — `execute` falls through and reports that one genuinely, and the *next* iteration sets `cancelled`, so no result is lost or invented.\n- **`94a68c3` (cancel curl instead of orphaning it)** — correct, including the process-group and backstop paths. SIGINT during a call to a never-answering endpoint: talaria exits 1 in <1 s, `pgrep -a curl` empty at t+1..t+5 s, capture directory removed. With a fake `curl` that ignores every timeout and spawns a grandchild, `--timeout 2` killed the whole group at 4.0 s (`MaxTime + killGrace`) with the grandchild gone, message `\"curl outlived its 2s timeout and was killed\"`. Repeating that with a ~1 MB inline body — so the stdin copy goroutine is blocked on a pipe whose reader never reads — still returned in 4.09 s with no leak, confirming `WaitDelay` unblocks it.\n- **`e0cceae` (serialise history appends so trim cannot destroy entries)** — correct across processes. Store seeded with 995 `call` entries (cap 1000), then 20 concurrent `talaria call` processes: final file exactly 1000 lines, all 20 new entries present, zero duplicate ids, oldest seeds evicted first. Also ran a `-race` test (in a throwaway copy of the repo at /tmp/talcopy, not the repo) with 16 goroutines appending 20 entries each interleaved with 8 goroutines reading — 320/320 entries, no duplicate ids, no race reported.\n\n### Other things checked that were clean\n\nNo deadlock on large payloads: 10 MB request body (temp-file path), ~1 MB body (inline config path) and a 50 MB response all completed in ≤1.4 s with no stray curl and no leftover `talaria-body-*` — stdout/stderr are `bytes.Buffer`s drained by os/exec's own goroutines concurrently with the stdin write, and the response body goes to a capture file rather than a pipe, so the classic pipe deadlock has no surface here. The `defer cleanupConfig()` zeroing of the config buffer cannot race the stdin copy goroutine: `Wait` always drains `c.goroutineErr` before returning, including on the WaitDelay path (`exec.go:868`). Per-operation `--timeout` behaves as DESIGN §4 requires — a wedged endpoint failed at 2.1 s with `curl exited 28` and the report for the other three operations was still produced. Spec-cache writes (internal/spec/source.go:156) are temp-file + rename and safe against a concurrent reader. `--body -` correctly reads the Go process's stdin (internal/request/body.go:95), never curl's, so the config document and the body are never spliced. `preflightOnce`/`preflightErr` (internal/curl/version.go:29) are safe — `sync.Once` supplies the happens-before, and 24 concurrent `ExecuteWith` calls under `-race` were clean. The app spawns no goroutines of its own, so there are no leak candidates outside os/exec.",
