    "content": [
      {
        "type": "tool_use",
        "id": "toolu_01Bh3Mpn33n6dYCn6W6w2EFH",
        "name": "Write",
        "input": {
          "file_path": "/home/casper/projects/talaria/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md",
          "content": "# Implementation plan — review round 3 fixes\n\nFixes the 8 CRIT findings in `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md`. WARN and INFO findings are recorded there\nfor a human and produce no tasks.\n\nSpec: `docs/design/DESIGN.md`. Every task keeps `gofmt`, `go vet` and `go test -race ./...`\nclean, and adds a regression test that fails before the fix.\n\n### Task 1: Scope a resolved credential to an allowed destination host\n\n**Fixes findings:** #1, #2\n\nA credential resolves for whatever host `--base-url`, the spec or a `history.jsonl` line\nnames. Both findings are the same missing check — nothing compares the destination against a\npolicy source outside the attacker's reach — so one gate serves both call sites.\n\nThe policy: the allowed host set is the profile's `base-url` host, plus the profile's\n`allow-hosts`, plus a config-file-level `allow-hosts`. When none of those exist, `call` falls\nback to the spec's `servers[*]` hosts (per finding #1's suggested fix (b)); when the spec\ndeclares no server either, the destination the caller states is the only one there is and is\nallowed, so the documented \"no server, pass `--base-url`\" path keeps working. `history replay`\nhas no spec, so it gets no spec fallback: with no profile and no config `allow-hosts`, a\nreplay that would resolve a credential is refused. Replays that resolve no credential are\nnever gated.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/config/config.go` (modify) — `AllowHosts []string` on `Profile` and on the\n  top-level config struct\n- `internal/config/hosts.go` (create) — the gate and its error\n- `internal/request/build.go` (modify)\n- `cmd/talaria/history.go` (modify)\n- `AGENT.md`, `README.md` (modify)\n- `internal/config/hosts_test.go`, `internal/request/build_test.go`,\n  `cmd/talaria/history_test.go` (create/modify)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Add `AllowHosts []string \\`yaml:\"allow-hosts\"\\`` to `config.Profile` and to the top-level\n   config struct in `internal/config/config.go`.\n2. In a new `internal/config/hosts.go`, write `AllowedHosts(cfg, prof) []string` returning the\n   profile `base-url` host plus both `allow-hosts` lists, and\n   `CheckHost(allowed []string, host, source string) error` returning a `clierr.Usage` (exit 2)\n   that names the refused host, the source that chose it (`--base-url`, the spec, the history\n   entry) and how to permit it (`allow-hosts`). Match on host only, case-folded, port included.\n3. In `internal/request/build.go`, after `baseURL()` picks a destination and before\n   `credentials()` attaches anything, compare the destination host against `AllowedHosts`\n   unioned with the hosts of every entry in `doc.Model.Servers` when that set is otherwise\n   empty. On a mismatch call `b.fail` with `CheckHost`'s wording so it lands as exit 2. Leave\n   the request unbuilt rather than building it credential-free.\n4. Allow the destination unconditionally when the allowed set is empty *and* the spec declares\n   no servers — otherwise `--base-url` against a serverless spec stops working.\n5. In `cmd/talaria/history.go`, gate `replayRequest` (:554): compute the allowed set from the\n   profile and config only, and before resolving any `<redacted:env:NAME>` reference check the\n   parsed host against it. Refuse with exit 2 naming the host. Keep the gate off the path that\n   replays no credential, so an unauthenticated replay is unaffected.\n6. Update `AGENT.md` (the `--base-url` bullet and the `history replay` paragraph at :248-251)\n   and `README.md`'s profile section (:174) to state that a profile pins destination as well as\n   auth, and document `allow-hosts`.\n\n**Verify:** `go test ./...`\n- [ ] `call --profile p --base-url <other-host>` exits 2 naming the refused host and sends\n      nothing, while `--base-url` matching the profile's host still works\n- [ ] A spec whose `servers[0].url` is outside a configured `allow-hosts` is refused\n- [ ] `--base-url` against a spec with no `servers` and no profile still works\n- [ ] `history replay` of an entry whose URL was edited to another host refuses with exit 2\n      rather than resolving `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER`\n- [ ] `history replay` of an entry carrying no credential still replays\n\n### Task 2: Carry parameter- and media-type-level examples through to `run`\n\n**Fixes findings:** #3\n\n`extract.go:166` keeps only `media.Schema`, and `operation.Param` has no example field, so the\ndocumented priority order (\"spec `example`/`examples` → fixtures → generated\") only ever sees\n`schema.example`. `run` fires invented ids and empty bodies at real APIs.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/operation/operation.go` (modify)\n- `internal/operation/extract.go` (modify)\n- `internal/gen/fixtures.go` (modify)\n- `internal/operation/extract_test.go`, `internal/gen/fixtures_test.go` (modify)\n- `internal/operation/testdata/` (modify — a fixture with examples at each level)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Add `Example any` and `Examples map[string]any` to `operation.MediaType` and\n   `operation.Param` in `internal/operation/operation.go`.\n2. In `extractContent` (`extract.go:159`), populate both from libopenapi's `MediaType.Example`\n   and `MediaType.Examples`, decoding the yaml nodes the same way `gen.decodeNode` does. Do the\n   same for `Parameter.Example`/`Parameter.Examples` in the parameter extractor.\n3. In `gen.paramValue` (`fixtures.go:172`), consult `p.Example`, then the first `p.Examples`\n   entry's `value`, before falling through to `exampleValue(schema)`. `examples` iteration must\n   be deterministic — sort by key or preserve spec order.\n4. In `gen.bodyFor` (`fixtures.go:200`), do the same for the selected media type before\n   `exampleValue(schema)`.\n5. Extend the fixture spec so a test asserts all four locations are honoured in the documented\n   order.\n\n**Verify:** `go test ./internal/operation/... ./internal/gen/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n- [ ] A parameter-level `example` is used for the path/query value\n- [ ] A media-type `example` is used as the request body\n- [ ] A media-type `examples` entry is used when there is no singular `example`\n- [ ] Schema-level `example` still wins over generation when no higher-priority example exists\n\n### Task 3: Make OAuth2 and OpenID Connect bring-your-own-token instead of uncallable\n\n**Fixes findings:** #4\n\n`schemeReason` refuses `type: oauth2` outright, so no `--header`, profile `auth:` or profile\n`headers:` entry can satisfy it, and `Schemes` drops it so `auth check` reports an empty list\nand exits 0 on a spec where every call exits 2. DESIGN.md §5 says \"bring your own token\"; there\nis no way to bring one.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/config/auth.go` (modify)\n- `internal/config/auth_test.go` (modify — including the existing `getOAuthOnly` and\n  `getOAuthOrBearer` expectations, which assert the current refusal)\n- `cmd/talaria/auth_test.go` (modify)\n- `docs/design/DESIGN.md` or `AGENT.md` (modify — state the mapping)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. In `credentialFor` (`auth.go:288`), treat `oauth2` and `openIdConnect` as bearer-shaped:\n   `Kind = KindBearer`, `In = InHeader`, `Name = \"Authorization\"`, `Ref = secret.Env(EnvBearer)`,\n   still overridable by a profile `auth:` entry via `profileRef`.\n2. Remove the `oauth2`/`openIdConnect` refusal from `schemeReason` (`auth.go:223`) so those\n   schemes reach `credentialFor`. Keep refusing genuinely unsupportable ones (an API key in a\n   place a request does not have, an unknown `type`).\n3. Confirm the existing missing-credential path now produces exit 5 naming\n   `$TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` rather than exit 2, per DESIGN.md §4.\n4. Update `TestResolvePrefersTheSupportedAlternative`-style tests: with oauth2 supported, the\n   `getOAuthOrBearer` fixture may now pick either — assert the documented preference explicitly\n   rather than relying on oauth2 being rejected.\n5. Ensure `Schemes` (`auth.go:179`) now includes them so `auth check` reports something. For any\n   scheme still refused, report it with `\"present\": false` and the `schemeReason` text so\n   `auth check` cannot exit 0 on a spec no operation can call.\n\n**Verify:** `go test ./internal/config/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n- [ ] `auth check` on an oauth2-only spec lists the scheme and names the variable to set\n- [ ] `call` on that spec with `$TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` unset exits 5 naming the variable\n- [ ] `call` with `$TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` set sends `Authorization: Bearer …`\n- [ ] A profile `auth: {petstore_auth: ${TOK}}` entry overrides the convention name\n\n### Task 4: Expand server variables and fall through on a relative server URL\n\n**Fixes findings:** #5\n\n`baseURL()` rejects `{scheme}://host:{port}/base` outright, so a whole class of standard 3.x\nspecs discovers fine and calls nothing. The same branch hard-fails on a relative `servers[].url`\ninstead of falling through to the \"no base URL … pass `--base-url`\" message its own doc comment\nat build.go:132-133 promises.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/request/build.go` (modify — `firstServer` and `baseURL`)\n- `internal/request/build_test.go` (modify)\n- `internal/request/testdata/` or the shared spec fixtures (modify)\n- `AGENT.md` (modify — the `--base-url` bullet)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. In `firstServer` (`build.go:172`), substitute each `{name}` in the server URL with the\n   matching `Server.Variables` entry's `Default` (libopenapi exposes `Variables` on\n   `v3high.Server`). A variable with no default, or a `{name}` with no declared variable, leaves\n   the URL unexpanded so the existing absoluteness check reports it.\n2. In `baseURL` (`build.go:134`), when a candidate parses as a *relative* URL (no scheme, no\n   host) sourced from the spec, skip it rather than failing — so the loop reaches the\n   \"no base URL: the spec declares no server, so pass --base-url or set one in a profile\"\n   message. Keep the hard failure for a candidate that is absolute-but-wrong (a `file://`\n   scheme, an unparseable string) and for a relative `--base-url`, which is a usage error.\n3. Add server variables to `AGENT.md`'s `--base-url` bullet so an agent hitting an unexpandable\n   one has a next step.\n\n**Verify:** `go test ./internal/request/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n- [ ] A spec with `{scheme}://127.0.0.1:{port}/base` and variable defaults builds a request\n      against `http://127.0.0.1:9977/base`\n- [ ] A variable with no default still fails with the \"not an absolute http(s) URL\" message\n- [ ] `servers: [{url: /v1}]` produces the \"no base URL … pass --base-url\" message, and\n      `--base-url` then works\n- [ ] Swagger 2.0 `basePath` handling is unchanged\n\n### Task 5: Sanitise cells in the TSV and pretty renderers\n\n**Fixes findings:** #6\n\n`tsvRenderer.Render` (`render.go:105`) joins untrusted spec text with `\\t` and `\\n` and escapes\nnothing, so a crafted `summary` forges a well-formed row and an agent doing\n`list --output tsv | cut -f3` gets an operationId no operation backs. Fixing it in the renderer\nrather than per producer is the same argument `junit.go:62-66` already makes.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/output/render.go` (modify)\n- `internal/output/render_test.go` (modify)\n- `cmd/talaria/run.go` (modify — the summary row at :590)\n- `cmd/talaria/list_test.go` or `cmd/talaria/run_test.go` (modify)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. In `tsvRenderer.Render`, replace `\\t`, `\\r` and `\\n` in every cell with a space (or `\\t`/`\\n`\n   escapes — pick one and state it in the doc comment) before joining.\n2. Do the same in `prettyRenderer.Render` (`render.go:117`), where the forged line renders too.\n   `tabwriter` needs the tabs stripped from cell content for its columns to be right anyway.\n3. At `cmd/talaria/run.go:590`, drop the single-cell summary row from the TSV rendering — or pad\n   it to the table's row width — so `run --report tsv` has a uniform field count. Keep the\n   summary line in `pretty`.\n4. Add a renderer test using a cell containing a tab and a newline, asserting the row count and\n   per-row field count are exactly the number of input rows and cells.\n\n**Verify:** `go test ./internal/output/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n- [ ] `list --output tsv` on a spec whose summary contains `\\n` and `\\t` emits one line per\n      operation, every line with the same field count\n- [ ] `run --report tsv` emits a uniform field count on every line\n- [ ] Existing TSV consumers (`list`, `search`, `uses`, `history`) are byte-identical for\n      well-behaved specs\n\n### Task 6: Cap generated string and array sizes so a hostile spec cannot OOM the process\n\n**Fixes findings:** #7\n\n`array()` does `make([]any, 0, count)` straight off `s.MinItems` and `clampLength` does\n`strings.Repeat(\"x\", int(*schema.MinLength)-len(s))`, both unbounded and both fed from the spec,\nwhich §1 says is untrusted. The failure is a Go runtime `fatal error`, so it bypasses `clierr`\nentirely: no `talaria/v1` envelope, and exit 2, which §4 defines as \"fix your invocation\" — an\nagent branching on the code retries forever.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/gen/gen.go` (modify)\n- `internal/gen/gen_test.go` (modify)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Add package constants `maxGeneratedItems = 100` and `maxGeneratedLength = 64 << 10` beside\n   `DefaultMaxDepth`, with a doc comment naming the untrusted-spec argument.\n2. In `array()` (`gen.go:171`), when the schema's `MinItems` exceeds `maxGeneratedItems`, return\n   `nil, false` — `run` already reports that as a skip with a reason — rather than clamping to a\n   value the server would reject. Otherwise cap `count` at `maxGeneratedItems` before the\n   `make`.\n3. In `clampLength()` (`gen.go:309`), do the same for `MinLength` against `maxGeneratedLength`.\n   `clampLength` returns a bare string, so either give it an `ok` return threaded through its\n   callers or clamp there and skip the field at the `value()` level — whichever keeps the caller\n   set small.\n4. Guard the `int()` conversions: compare the `int64` against the cap *before* converting, so a\n   32-bit build cannot produce a negative length and panic inside `strings.Repeat`.\n5. Add tests for `minItems: 200000000` and `minLength: 600000000` asserting the field is skipped\n   with a reason and the process does not allocate.\n\n**Verify:** `go test ./internal/gen/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n- [ ] `run` on a spec with `minLength: 600000000` completes, reporting a skip with a reason and\n      a valid `talaria/v1` envelope\n- [ ] Same for `minItems: 200000000`\n- [ ] A schema with `minItems: 3` still generates 3 elements, and `minLength: 20` still pads\n\n### Task 7: Make a history append O(1) in the steady state\n\n**Fixes findings:** #8\n\n`Append` reads and parses the whole store twice, builds a third copy in memory and writes a\nfourth, all inside the cross-process flock — and at the retention cap that happens on every\ncall, forever. Measured 1.02 s and 253 MB RSS per call at the design's own maximum, ~2 GB RSS\nfor a 10-operation `run`, and 9.1 s of cross-process starvation for a concurrent invocation.\n\n**Files:**\n- `internal/corpus/store.go` (modify)\n- `internal/corpus/store_test.go` (modify)\n- callers of `Append` in `cmd/talaria/` (modify — context plumbing)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Derive the entry id without reading the store: timestamp plus a random suffix, wide enough\n   that a collision is not a practical concern. Delete `uniqueID`/`storedIDs` and the\n   whole-file read they do. Keep the existing \"ids are unique\" test — it should still pass.\n2. Amortise `trim`: only rewrite when a source is over `maxPerSource` by a slack margin\n   (e.g. 10%), so the steady state is a plain append and the rewrite happens once per ~100\n   calls instead of every call.\n3. Stream the rewrite: `bufio.Scanner` over the old file into the temp file, instead of\n   `os.ReadFile` plus a full `bytes.Buffer` copy. Peak memory becomes the largest single entry.\n   Note `MaxBody` is 64 KiB, so raise the scanner's buffer above the 64 KiB default.\n4. Thread the command's context into `Append` (`Append(ctx, e)`) and abandon the lock wait and\n   the rewrite when it is done, so SIGINT is not ignored for seconds on the corpus path.\n5. Keep the existing concurrency guarantees: the id, the write and the trim stay one critical\n   section, and the 20-concurrent-process test at the cap must still produce exactly 1000 lines\n   with zero duplicate ids, clean under `-race`.\n\n**Verify:** `go test -race ./internal/corpus/... ./cmd/talaria/...`\n- [ ] The 20-concurrent-process test at the retention cap still yields exactly 1000 lines, no\n      duplicate ids, oldest evicted first\n- [ ] A store at the cap keeps at most `maxPerSource` + slack entries per source\n- [ ] Appending to a store already at the cap performs no whole-file rewrite in the common case\n- [ ] A cancelled context makes `Append` return without rewriting\n"
        },
        "caller": {
          "type": "direct"
        }
      }
